window.StrictDoc.search.nodesByMid = {"1":{"MID":"922a9903febe482ebb00cf95fdb4769f","UID":"SEC-IRS-PY","TITLE":"Python package interfaces","_LINK":"SEC-IRS-PY"},"2":{"MID":"4849710027b5431db90f5a7dc9aff2bb","STATEMENT":"This Interface Requirements Specification (IRS) covers Python public APIs and the\nphysical interfaces of the patented plant that those APIs must expose as named\ninputs, outputs, and couplings: air, electrolyte, electrical tabs, stacking\ncontacts, and charge/discharge electrode selection (US12308414B2 and EP4602674A1).\n","_LINK":"4849710027b5431db90f5a7dc9aff2bb"},"3":{"MID":"440e2437a06649c8bba51382dc49a802","UID":"IA-IRS-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Public package imports","STATEMENT":"The following imports SHALL succeed after editable install:\n\n  import ironair\n  from ironair import constants\n  from ironair import chemistry\n  from ironair import components\n  from ironair import simulation\n  from ironair import scenarios\n  from ironair import plotting\n","COMMENT":"tests/test_package_imports.py passes in the project virtual environment.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-001"},"4":{"MID":"378e151392da42ddb944af1589376c83","UID":"IA-IRS-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"RHS signature","STATEMENT":"Every dynamic component SHALL expose:\n\n  def rhs(self, t, y, inputs, context) -> numpy.ndarray\n\nt is time in seconds, y is a 1-D float64 state vector, inputs is a mapping of named\nphysical inputs, and context carries shared couplings without hidden mutation of\ncomponent parameters.\n","COMMENT":"Signature inspection and a deterministic double-evaluation test pass for each component.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-002"},"5":{"MID":"3384e63648d04f4ab4729f20800cc74b","UID":"IA-IRS-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"CoupledSystem simulate interface","STATEMENT":"ironair.simulation.CoupledSystem.simulate SHALL accept t_span, optional t_eval,\noptional inputs callable, solver method, and tolerances, and SHALL return a\nSimulationResult.\n\nt_span SHALL accept LODES windows including 8 h, 100 h (3.6e5 s), 300 h, and\nmulti-day values from US12308414B2 without changing parameter objects.\n","COMMENT":"Default method is BDF or Radau. Result includes t, y, state_names, and diagnostics.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-003"},"6":{"MID":"ff7551c65d6e40acb125a0ec3c1388da","UID":"IA-IRS-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Plotting input types","STATEMENT":"ironair.plotting functions SHALL accept SimulationResult or pandas.DataFrame.\n\nPlot metadata SHALL be able to carry scenario identifier, patent source citation,\nand operating assumptions (electrolyte molarity, temperature, architecture mode).\n","COMMENT":"Both input types produce a matplotlib Figure.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-004"},"7":{"MID":"11e116e252854f80a3e653b1877d0c90","UID":"IA-IRS-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Estimator measurement-only interface","STATEMENT":"BatteryStateEstimator SHALL accept measurements and prior estimates only. It SHALL\nNOT receive the plant hidden state vector during observer execution.\n","COMMENT":"Type hints and tests pass a MeasurementVector, not the plant y, into the estimator.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-005"},"8":{"MID":"6a1513174b96469da38c09a5ca78a23c","UID":"SEC-IRS-PHYS","TITLE":"Patented physical interfaces","_LINK":"SEC-IRS-PHYS"},"9":{"MID":"8b6586c7eeaf415590d163723654315d","UID":"IA-IRS-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Air interface","STATEMENT":"AirSystem and OxygenReduction SHALL exchange named flows for dry air or oxygen,\nhumidity, CO2 mole fraction, pO2, volumetric or mass flow, and manifold pressure.\n\nORR SHALL expose an electrolyte-facing first surface and an air-facing second\nsurface (EP4602674A1 Claim 1). Stacked submerged ORR sections SHALL expose per-\nsection pressure drop so that Claim 1 equal-dP can be enforced.\n\nAirflow regulation structures including holed ramp arrays (US12308414B2 Claims 14-15)\nSHALL appear as named hydraulic parameters, not as undocumented magic numbers.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-006"},"10":{"MID":"77489320595044f4835ec5ccfe5cdda2","UID":"IA-IRS-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte and hydraulic interface","STATEMENT":"Electrolyte, Reservoir, Pump, Valve, and Pipe SHALL exchange water, KOH/NaOH/LiOH,\ncarbonate, dissolved oxygen, liquid volume, temperature, and leak flows.\n\nPrimary-to-secondary containment leak into vessel cavity 161 (US12308414B2\nFIGS. 1E-1F) SHALL be a named leak path. Shared electrolyte baths of multiple\ncells SHALL use a common electrolyte context key.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-007"},"11":{"MID":"0124e5a8471741a5b1da75d8ac832fc8","UID":"IA-IRS-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrical tab and stacking interface","STATEMENT":"Iron, ORR, and OER components SHALL expose named electrical ports for anode tabs,\nOER cathode tabs, ORR tabs, can-negative housing, and stacking contact points\n(US12308414B2 Claims 3 and 5; EP4602674A1 FIG. 8 tabs 230, 240a, 240b).\n\nCharge and discharge paths SHALL be distinct named connections selectable by\nSupervisoryController. Bus bars outside the active-area plane (EP4602674A1\nClaims 30-31) SHALL be identifiable in the state-index map.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-008"},"12":{"MID":"b9b18369f79f44e096e6d14d3dc6edfd","UID":"IA-IRS-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Channel and interdigitation interface","STATEMENT":"IronElectrode SHALL publish channel occupancy, ChanFrac, rib width, and whether\neach channel contains OER, additive, both, or neither (EP4602674A1 Claims 5-6).\n\nOxygenEvolution SHALL publish projection length, trunk presence, corrugated versus\ndiscrete-cathode layout, and iron-OER gap. Separator SHALL publish ionic\nresistance and bubble-egress availability between those ports.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-009"},"13":{"MID":"2249bc2d99ce4c209277ae9b467aa542","UID":"IA-IRS-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Scenario and architecture selection interface","STATEMENT":"ironair.scenarios SHALL accept an architecture identifier covering US12308414B2\nClaim 1 stacked ORR, Claim 18 DRI bed, floating ORR, and EP4602674A1 Claims 1, 20,\n38, 55, 70, and 77 layouts.\n\nInvalid combinations (for example bifunctional ORR forced to OER potential while\nisolation is required) SHALL raise a documented configuration error.\n","_LINK":"IA-IRS-010"},"14":{"MID":"16ba73a314b9490ab43dbec3f9766906","UID":"SEC-SDD-SRC","TITLE":"Source layout and clean baseline","_LINK":"SEC-SDD-SRC"},"15":{"MID":"8af09c7a84134826b1e7d144a963f7dd","STATEMENT":"This Software Design Document (SDD) describes how ironair maps the patented\niron-air plant onto Python packages, DynamicComponent classes, and algebraic\nversus dynamic state partitions. Physical geometry and chemistry come from\nUS12308414B2 (device architectures for metal-air batteries) and EP4602674A1\n(electrode configurations for iron-air electrochemical systems). This SDD does\nnot implement a second reaction network besides ironair.chemistry.\n","_LINK":"8af09c7a84134826b1e7d144a963f7dd"},"16":{"MID":"da7864b1ec4b4d66969ef512d75836ef","UID":"IA-SDD-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Source layout","STATEMENT":"Package sources SHALL live under src/ironair as specified by IA-SYS-001.\n\nThis SDRS src/ layout is the project-specific design and SHALL be used even though\na generic Cursor rule discourages src/ layouts.\n\nPatent-derived parameters, architecture enumerations, and claim citations SHALL\nlive in typed parameter objects under the same package, not in notebooks.\n","COMMENT":"pyproject.toml configures setuptools package discovery with where = [\"src\"].\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-001"},"17":{"MID":"3de4ff05beb34fc48cfae571e04fc3f5","UID":"IA-SDD-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Clean Python baseline","STATEMENT":"This Python package is a new baseline. It SHALL NOT import, translate, or recover\ngoverning equations from prior MATLAB or Simulink implementations.\n\nPatent text SHALL be paraphrased into parameter metadata and requirement\nstatements. Governing equations SHALL be implemented from the shared chemistry\nand component protocols, not by copying copyrighted patent prose into source.\n","COMMENT":"Source tree contains no .m or .slx physics. Chemistry and component modules implement\nthe SDRS reaction set and ODE protocol directly.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-005"},"18":{"MID":"e3837ef7113c4919858286ff9558e2b8","UID":"SEC-SDD-PROTO","TITLE":"Component protocol and chemistry ownership","_LINK":"SEC-SDD-PROTO"},"19":{"MID":"50db4f0957ae464e9bd32d9fa5ec6923","UID":"IA-SDD-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Dynamic component base","STATEMENT":"All physical components SHALL implement ironair.ode.protocol.DynamicComponentProtocol\nand MAY inherit ironair.ode.component.DynamicComponent for shared validation.\n\nrhs(t, y, inputs, context) SHALL be a pure function of its arguments for a fixed\nparameter configuration.\n\nArchitecture flags (floating ORR, stacked submerged ORR, interdigitated OER,\nDRI bed, spiral, pleated) SHALL be parameters, not hidden rhs mutation.\n","COMMENT":"Protocol checks and unit tests cover every registered component class.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-002"},"20":{"MID":"d914903c08c04951bff86070b1fa4b73","UID":"IA-SDD-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Shared chemistry ownership","STATEMENT":"Electrode components SHALL obtain stoichiometric rates from ironair.chemistry rather\nthan embedding a second reaction network.\n\nIron conversion Fe / Fe(OH)2 / Fe3O4, ORR, OER, and HER SHALL use the capacities\nand half-reactions of IA-SYS-020 (EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0044]; US12308414B2 ORR\nfour-electron reaction). Faraday mapping SHALL convert electrode current to molar\nrate.\n","COMMENT":"Iron, ORR, OER, and HER rate mapping uses ReactionNetwork.S and Faraday conversion.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-003"},"21":{"MID":"8102205ac6a44771b3c20950709c0fbc","UID":"IA-SDD-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Algebraic versus dynamic states","STATEMENT":"Instantaneous constitutive laws (Ohmic drop, hydraulic orifice flow at a fixed\nposition, Nernst potential, stacked-ORR equal pressure drop of US12308414B2\nClaim 1, Faraday current-to-rate maps) SHALL be algebraic.\n\nDynamic states SHALL represent stored mass, charge, thermal energy, momentum, or\nexplicit controller memory, including iron phase inventories, electrolyte\nspecies, gas pockets, HER/OER bubble holdup, and controller modes.\n\nArtificial lag SHALL NOT be added to algebraic laws.\n","COMMENT":"CurrentCollector resistance is algebraic. CurrentCollector temperature is dynamic.\nEqualized ORR stack pressure drop is algebraic in geometry and flow.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-004"},"22":{"MID":"0ff6b69f382d47cbae14dc984fd8dea5","UID":"SEC-SDD-MAP","TITLE":"Mapping patented architectures onto components","_LINK":"SEC-SDD-MAP"},"23":{"MID":"9c53f0a6b3c3460b8bd885f286c68684","STATEMENT":"US12308414B2 Claim 1 stacked submerged ORR is IronAirCell plus GasDiffusionLayer\nplus AirSystem with a vertical stack of ORR sections. EP4602674A1 Claim 1\nchanneled iron plus interdigitated OER is IronElectrode plus OxygenEvolution with\nchannel geometry parameters. Dual-electrode switching is SupervisoryController\nmode plus algebraic electrical connection, not a second chemistry module.\n","_LINK":"9c53f0a6b3c3460b8bd885f286c68684"},"24":{"MID":"81c17a881858403a9ae8e0dafc1bbaa8","UID":"IA-SDD-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Dual-electrode electrical design","STATEMENT":"IronAirCell SHALL expose separate ORR and OER electrical ports. SupervisoryController\nSHALL connect iron-OER-source in charge and iron-ORR-load in discharge\n(EP4602674A1 [0049]; US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B).\n\nWhen isolated, ORR potential SHALL NOT be forced to the OER operating potential.\nBifunctional single-air-electrode mode SHALL be an explicit reduced-order flag.\n","RATIONALE":"Protects carbon/PTFE ORR layers from OER potentials (US12308414B2). Matches\nEP4602674A1 switch 116.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-006"},"25":{"MID":"0501b6edaeff40a18a6d98442a290a21","UID":"IA-SDD-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron electrode geometric modes","STATEMENT":"IronElectrode SHALL implement at least these named geometric modes:\n\n- packed DRI pellet bed (US12308414B2 Claim 18);\n- planar porous electrode (EP4602674A1 FIG. 1 control);\n- channeled rib-and-spine electrode (Claim 1, FIG. 2);\n- discrete anode array (Claim 20);\n- spiral with current collector (Claim 70);\n- pleated electrode (Claim 77).\n\nMode-specific parameters SHALL include loading 1-7 g Fe/cm2, electrolyte volume\nfraction 0.5-0.9, channel length/width/spacing, ChanFrac, and spine/rib porosity.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-007"},"26":{"MID":"fa375c1798f54a69b87b4f664fb237eb","UID":"IA-SDD-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"ORR geometric modes","STATEMENT":"OxygenReduction and GasDiffusionLayer SHALL implement floating, vertical\nnatural-air-breathing, inverse box/tube, stacked submerged equal-dP, wavy/rippled,\nhelical, and coaxial-core modes listed in IA-SYS-012.\n\nAirSystem SHALL supply cascading bubbles, regulated chamber pressure, or ambient\nheadspace as required by the selected mode. Claim 1 equal pressure drop SHALL be\nan algebraic hydraulic constraint across stacked sections.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-008"},"27":{"MID":"32ce613d0a874b81b79f72d719d45964","UID":"IA-SDD-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Vessel and lid design mapping","STATEMENT":"BatteryEnclosure SHALL represent inner wall, outer wall, cavity, optional bag-in-box,\nstacking contacts, and can-negative conduction (US12308414B2 Claims 3 and 5;\nFIGS. 1E-1F, 11).\n\nLid functions (air distribution, vent, thermal, current collection, sensing, DC\nlinks) SHALL map to AirSystem, ThermalNetwork, CurrentCollector, DynamicSensor,\nand DCBus ports rather than a monolithic undocumented object.\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-009"},"28":{"MID":"c4826b548b9947c4ade11b3b8e7b51ac","UID":"IA-SDD-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Spatial discretization of thick electrodes","STATEMENT":"When high-fidelity mode is selected, IronElectrode SHALL discretize through-plane\nor in-plane ionic path length so that front-versus-back HER competition of\nEP4602674A1 [0046]-[0047] is representable.\n\nInterdigitated OER SHALL shorten the modeled ionic path to the entire iron area\nrelative to planar OER at the same loading (FIGS. 19-24).\n","_LINK":"IA-SDD-010"},"29":{"MID":"55c9b31f2ec643eb8c83cac56c64bcf0","UID":"SEC-SRS-PURPOSE","TITLE":"Purpose, sources, and software boundary","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-PURPOSE"},"30":{"MID":"a74eeaf4046d4c11878405d4042ed731","STATEMENT":"This System Requirements Specification (SRS) defines the iron-air-modeling plant\nthat the ironair Python package SHALL simulate. Physical architecture, chemistry,\nelectrolyte, air-electrode, iron-electrode, vessel, and grid-storage operating\nenvelopes are derived from two Form Energy disclosures:\n\n- US12308414B2, Device architectures for metal-air batteries (granted 2025-05-20;\n  Appl. 16/913,877; published family WO2020264344A1 / EP3991234A1 / CN114207915A).\n- EP4602674A1, Electrode configurations for iron-air electrochemical systems\n  (published 2025-08-20; Appl. EP23878232.0; WO2024081792A1; priority US 63/379,223).\n\nThe patents overlap as a dual-electrode iron-air family: separate oxygen reduction\nreaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) positives, an iron-containing\nnegative, and alkaline aqueous electrolyte. US12308414B2 emphasizes vessel,\nsubmerged/stacked ORR, air delivery, and DRI-pellet anodes. EP4602674A1 emphasizes\nchanneled/interdigitated iron and OER electrodes for thick, high-areal-capacity\nanodes. Where they differ, both configurations SHALL be modelable.\n","_LINK":"a74eeaf4046d4c11878405d4042ed731"},"31":{"MID":"84c9973c5ff24a74825cbce53facd50d","STATEMENT":"The software is an installable Python package that reconstructs the patented plant\nas coupled ODE/DAE components. Passing tests verifies the software against this\nSRS; it does not constitute experimental hardware validation of US12308414B2 or\nEP4602674A1.\n","_LINK":"84c9973c5ff24a74825cbce53facd50d"},"32":{"MID":"5ea65a3bad3d404c91c9154023f7d5d5","UID":"IA-SYS-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Python package","STATEMENT":"The software SHALL be implemented as an installable Python package named ironair.\n\nAll public APIs SHALL be importable using:\n\n  import ironair\n  from ironair import constants\n  from ironair import chemistry\n  from ironair import components\n  from ironair import simulation\n  from ironair import scenarios\n  from ironair import plotting\n\nThe implementation SHALL use a standard src/ package layout.\n\nThe project SHALL provide editable installation using:\n\n  pip install -e \".[dev,notebooks]\"\n","RATIONALE":"A single installable package is the integration boundary for notebooks, tests, and\ncoupled simulation of the patented iron-air plant. The SDRS src/ layout takes\nprecedence over the generic no-src-directory Cursor rule for this project.\n","COMMENT":"Package ironair installs in a virtual environment. The listed imports succeed after\npip install -e \".[dev,notebooks]\" or the equivalent uv installation.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-001"},"33":{"MID":"bbb1f0a6718d4bb7920252599f68b173","UID":"IA-SYS-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Patent-grounded plant of record","STATEMENT":"The simulated plant of record SHALL be a rechargeable aqueous alkaline iron-air\nbattery with a dual-electrode air side (distinct ORR discharge cathode and OER\ncharge cathode), an iron-containing metal electrode, and a liquid electrolyte that\nseparates the air-side electrodes from the metal electrode.\n\nThe plant SHALL be consistent with US12308414B2 Claim 1 (ORR, OER, metal electrode,\nand electrolyte) and with EP4602674A1 Claim 1 (iron electrode, ORR electrode with\nair-facing surface, interdigitated OER, and contacting electrolyte).\n\nPrimary-only (ORR-only air electrode) operation MAY be enabled as a documented\nreduced-order mode but SHALL be identified as non-rechargeable relative to the\nsecondary dual-electrode plant.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Summary and FIG. 1A define metal-air cells with anode, cathode,\nelectrolyte, and optional gaseous headspace. EP4602674A1 [0048]-[0049] and FIG. 1\ndefine dual-electrode iron-air cells with switchable charge (OER) and discharge\n(ORR) paths.\n","COMMENT":"Family note: US12308414B2 is the granted US member of WO2020264344. EP4602674A1 is\nthe EP publication of WO2024081792 and is not a literal equivalent of US12308414B2;\nit is a later electrode-geometry disclosure in the same iron-air product family.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-006"},"34":{"MID":"dc4a4a9211684e6bacb369be7ba17b98","UID":"SEC-SRS-MISSION","TITLE":"Mission, duration, and grid context","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-MISSION"},"35":{"MID":"909d32c05e734a47a35a23ef03edd406","STATEMENT":"Both patents frame iron-air cells as long-duration and ultra-long-duration energy\nstorage (LODES) for electric power grids. US12308414B2 Background states that\nexisting storage covers milliseconds to hours and that systems of at least 8 h are\nneeded. The detailed description defines long/ultra-long duration as 8 h or longer,\nincluding 8-20 h, 20-24 h, a week, and seasonal (days to months to a year) storage\nof surplus solar generation. EP4602674A1 [0002] restates the greater-than-8 h need.\n","_LINK":"909d32c05e734a47a35a23ef03edd406"},"36":{"MID":"a9c3ed0b31e34173a4de077dfe67a488","UID":"IA-SYS-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Long-duration energy storage mission","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL model grid-connected LODES operation with energy storage durations\nof 8 hours or longer, including 8 h, 8-20 h, 20 h, 20-24 h, 24 h, week-scale, and\nseasonal (multi-day to multi-month) windows as defined in US12308414B2 (long\nduration / ultra-long duration; LODES).\n\nAnode thickness and independent charge versus discharge length scales SHALL be\nparameterizable so that example durations disclosed in US12308414B2 can be\nrepresented: about 3-5 cm anode thickness for about 100 h; about 4-6 cm for about\n300 h; and an asymmetric example of about 100 h charge with about 300 h discharge\nfor a 5 cm by 5 cm square anode cross-section.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Definitions and Detailed Description (anode thickness versus duration;\nsymmetric versus asymmetric duration). EP4602674A1 [0002] and [0045] motivate thick\nhigh-areal-capacity iron electrodes to amortize air-electrode cost.\n","COMMENT":"Default demonstration discharge of 100 h (3.6e5 s) is the short end of the patented\nLODES range, not a claim that hardware has been validated at that duration.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-007"},"37":{"MID":"37c58e68bc2449e3905fe82d7cc3b0b2","UID":"IA-SYS-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Integrated physics simulation","STATEMENT":"The software SHALL model the complete iron-air battery as a coupled multiphysics\nsystem corresponding to the patented plant.\n\nThe model SHALL include metallic iron electrode, iron hydroxide and oxide phases,\noxygen reduction electrode, oxygen evolution electrode, hydrogen evolution,\nalkaline electrolyte, electrolyte transport, separator, gas diffusion layer,\ncurrent collectors, cell assembly, thermal system, air intake, fan/blower, gas\nmanifolds, ventilation, electrolyte reservoir, circulation pump, piping, valves,\nheat exchanger, battery stack, battery module, battery enclosure, DC electrical\ndistribution, DC/DC conversion, grid inverter, transformer, grid interface,\nmeasurement sensors, battery management, supervisory control, fault management,\nstate estimation, and degradation and aging.\n\nThese components SHALL interact through shared physical quantities and conserved\nmaterial, charge, and energy flows.\n\nVessel, lid, and air-delivery functions disclosed in US12308414B2 (electrolyte\ncontainment, secondary containment, thermal management cavity, air distribution,\nprocess-gas venting, current collection, sensing, DC connections) SHALL map onto\nthe enclosure, air-system, thermal, collector, and control components listed\nabove.\n","RATIONALE":"Balance-of-plant and grid interfaces determine operating envelopes of the\nelectrochemical cell. US12308414B2 FIGS. 1E-1F, 9A, and 94-102 describe\nmulti-function vessels, lids, and bulk energy storage systems. EP4602674A1 FIG. 1\nadds the dual-electrode switch between power source (charge) and load (discharge).\n","COMMENT":"Every listed component has a DynamicComponent implementation that can be assembled\ninto ironair.simulation.CoupledSystem and exchanges named physical flows.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-002"},"38":{"MID":"584adb73100c4d5c8e8bcb21c4730433","UID":"IA-SYS-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Dual-electrode charge and discharge paths","STATEMENT":"During charge the model SHALL electrically connect the iron electrode to the OER\nelectrode and a power source so that iron species are reduced toward metallic\nFe(0).\n\nDuring discharge the model SHALL electrically connect the iron electrode to the\nORR electrode and a load so that metallic iron is oxidized toward higher-valence\nspecies including Fe(OH)2 and Fe3O4.\n\nA mode switch SHALL select charge versus discharge electrical paths as in\nEP4602674A1 [0049] switch 116 and US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B toggle switch 550.\n\nOER and ORR electrodes SHALL be allowed to be electrically separated so that the\nORR electrode is not exposed to high OER operating potentials that can damage or\noxidize the ORR electrode (US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0048]-[0049] and US12308414B2 dual-cathode architecture. Independent\nOER/ORR materials and geometries are separately optimized (US12308414B2).\n","COMMENT":"The supervisory controller implements the switch as a discrete operating mode, not\nas a hidden mutation of plant parameters.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-008"},"39":{"MID":"dd087e2a0755425baeb87560b807ce62","UID":"SEC-SRS-US-ARCH","TITLE":"US12308414B2 device architecture (granted claims)","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-US-ARCH"},"40":{"MID":"bb3e05e5415e40d1b29d6561bf77b059","STATEMENT":"US12308414B2 Claim 1 is the granted independent claim: a battery with ORR electrode,\nOER electrode, metal electrode, and electrolyte separating the air-side electrodes\nfrom the metal electrode, wherein the ORR electrode is submerged, comprises a\nseries of discrete ORR sections stacked vertically below the electrolyte surface,\neach section geometry varies with depth, and pressure drop across each stacked\nsection is the same in the depth direction. Claims 2-18 add dual electrodes,\nstackable vessels, wavy/rippled ORR, can-negative housing, inverse-air frames and\nboxes, supplied air, curved/tube ORR, movable metal electrode, airflow ramps,\nairflow-flex electrical separation, porous ORR separator, and DRI pellet beds.\n","_LINK":"bb3e05e5415e40d1b29d6561bf77b059"},"41":{"MID":"b5b298483d9f4f48ba366bcbb1493c78","UID":"IA-SYS-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Submerged stacked ORR with depth-equalized pressure drop","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL support a submerged ORR electrode comprising a series of discrete\nORR electrode sections arranged vertically in a stack below the electrolyte\nsurface.\n\nEach discrete ORR section SHALL have geometry that can vary with depth below the\nelectrolyte surface.\n\nThe air-path model SHALL be able to impose equal pressure drop across each stacked\nORR section in the depth direction.\n\nThis configuration SHALL be the default representation of US12308414B2 Claim 1.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claim 1. Detailed description FIGS. 25A-36B, 57-58, and 35A-35C\ndescribe stacked-core / distillation-column ORR layers with depth-dependent bubble\nthickness, baffles, and cascading air.\n","COMMENT":"Equal pressure drop is an algebraic hydraulic constitutive law, not an artificial\ndynamic lag (IA-SYS-003).\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-009"},"42":{"MID":"fbf424f9499046578e6f489dc4831614","UID":"IA-SYS-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Separate ORR and OER electrodes","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL model ORR and OER as separate electrodes (US12308414B2 Claim 2)\nand SHALL also support a documented bifunctional single air electrode in which ORR\nand OER are portions of the same electrode (US12308414B2 Summary).\n\nWhen separate, both electrodes MAY be submerged (Claim 6).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 2 and 6; Summary dual-electrode versus bifunctional options.\nEP4602674A1 uses the dual-electrode configuration as the baseline plant (FIG. 1).\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-010"},"43":{"MID":"cb4eb02d5ab1416da594654fbcf2758e","UID":"IA-SYS-011","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stackable vessel and secondary containment","STATEMENT":"The enclosure model SHALL represent a stackable vessel that supports ORR, OER,\nmetal electrode, and electrolyte and is configured to self-stack on other vessels\n(US12308414B2 Claim 3).\n\nThe vessel SHALL be modelable as a double-walled container with an inner wall\n(primary electrolyte vessel), outer wall (secondary containment), and cavity used\nfor thermal-management fluid, electrical isolation, thermal mass, or temporary\nelectrolyte hold during maintenance (US12308414B2 FIGS. 1E-1F).\n\nA bag-in-box vessel (structural box plus electrolyte bag) SHALL be an allowed\nreduced-order containment option.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claim 3 and vessel description. Self-stacking increases plant power\ndensity (MW/acre). Conductive contact points allow stacked cells in series.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-011"},"44":{"MID":"67fe727e3f354149a65c11b8600ee22b","UID":"IA-SYS-012","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"ORR geometry and inverse air-breathing options","STATEMENT":"The ORR component SHALL support the following patented geometries as selectable\nconfigurations, each identified in metadata:\n\n- wavy or rippled ORR to increase triple-phase boundary at a given footprint\n  (US12308414B2 Claim 4; FIGS. 10A-10C);\n- frame that displaces electrolyte to create a gas volume at depth (Claim 7);\n- box configuration filled with air (Claim 8; FIGS. 14-16 inverse air-breathing);\n- supplied-air ORR (Claim 9);\n- curved ORR (Claim 10);\n- frame trapping supplied air in an air chamber bounded by the ORR surface\n  (Claim 11);\n- tube-shaped ORR, including circular, oval, teardrop, or polygonal tubes\n  (Claim 12; FIGS. 20A-23, 59-62);\n- floating ORR at the electrolyte free surface with submerged OER (Summary;\n  FIGS. 1B-3);\n- vertical natural-air-breathing ORR (FIGS. 4A-8);\n- convex oxygen-pocket submerged ORR (FIGS. 17-19B);\n- open-bottom inverse air-breathing ORR (FIG. 24);\n- helical/auger ORR (FIG. 44);\n- rectilinear parallel-plate ORR stacks (FIGS. 45-47B);\n- coaxial cylindrical ORR core with hexagonal anode/OER tube (FIGS. 27-34).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 4 and 7-12 plus detailed-description architectures. Inverse\nair-breathing submerges ORR so leaks remain internal to the cell.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-012"},"45":{"MID":"f9ae3be8812c4a62bdefd187587a8eaa","UID":"IA-SYS-013","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Can-negative housing and DRI pellet anode","STATEMENT":"The metal electrode SHALL be modelable as a bed of direct reduced iron (DRI)\npellets or marbles (US12308414B2 Claim 18; FIG. 3 packed bed).\n\nA housing-as-current-conductor (can-negative) configuration SHALL be supported in\nwhich the vessel conducts anode current (Claim 5; FIG. 11). Housing materials\nSHALL include polymers (polyethylene, HDPE, UHMW, polypropylene) and metals\n(nickel, steel, anodized aluminum, nickel-coated steel or aluminum) as parameters.\n\nThe metal electrode position relative to ORR and OER SHALL be changeable\n(Claim 13; FIG. 52 movable anode).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 5, 13, and 18. DRI beds provide low-cost iron inventory for\nLODES. Can-negative stacked housings enable gravity-driven series connections.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-013"},"46":{"MID":"614c88ef33eb4ee091413266467211db","UID":"IA-SYS-014","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Airflow regulation and ORR separator","STATEMENT":"The air system SHALL include airflow regulation structures (US12308414B2 Claim 14),\nincluding arrays of ramps configured with holes (Claim 15).\n\nThe ORR electrode SHALL optionally include a porous separator (Claim 17), with\npore sizes in the 1 micrometer to 1 centimeter range when wrapping stacked-core\nORR columns (FIG. 36A-36B) to retain gas and limit anode contact with oxygen.\n\nOER and ORR SHALL be electrically separable by presence or absence of airflow that\nflexes and displaces the ORR electrode (Claim 16).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 14-17. Porous wrap prevents horizontal bubble escape and\noxygen-induced self-discharge of adjacent metal anodes.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-014"},"47":{"MID":"86555f52e084477c8bb4481269f8388d","UID":"SEC-SRS-EP-ARCH","TITLE":"EP4602674A1 electrode configurations (published claims)","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-EP-ARCH"},"48":{"MID":"0869affd707a43de8f1f0e23846bad2b","STATEMENT":"EP4602674A1 addresses thick iron electrodes whose ionic path length otherwise\ndegrades coulombic and voltage efficiency. Independent claims 1, 20, 38, 55, 70,\nand 77 cover channeled iron with interdigitated OER, discrete anode arrays,\ndiscrete cathode arrays, spiral bifilar iron/OER, and pleated iron/OER. Claim 86\ncovers a method of forming the Claim 1-19 assembly. The disclosure is pending at\nthe EPO and is treated as design intent for the model, not as a granted US claim\nset.\n","_LINK":"0869affd707a43de8f1f0e23846bad2b"},"49":{"MID":"ed6d7c30b7564139bd48999979b0b28c","UID":"IA-SYS-015","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Channeled iron electrode with interdigitated OER","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL model an iron electrode in contact with an anode current collector\nand comprising a plurality of channels; an ORR electrode with a first surface\nfacing the channels and an opposing second surface in contact with air; an OER\nelectrode interdigitated with the channels, with at least a portion of the OER\ndisposed within the channels in a direction perpendicular to the ORR plane; and\nelectrolyte in contact with the iron electrode, the ORR first surface, the\nchannels, and the OER electrode.\n\nThis configuration SHALL be the default representation of EP4602674A1 Claim 1\n(FIG. 2).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 Claim 1 and [0051]-[0058]. Channels carry ionic current while denser\nribs carry electronic current, decoupling porosity trade-offs ([0050]).\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-015"},"50":{"MID":"14420e30d08a4be78c13d86fe43e1e15","UID":"IA-SYS-016","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Channel and OER projection geometry","STATEMENT":"Channel and OER geometry parameters SHALL include the ranges disclosed in\nEP4602674A1:\n\n- channel average length 3 mm to 50 mm perpendicular to the ORR plane, with\n  nested examples 15-35 mm, 15-30 mm, and 15-25 mm (Claim 7; [0053]);\n- channel average width 1 mm to 40 mm parallel to the ORR plane, with nested\n  examples 2-30 mm, 2-20 mm, and 2-15 mm (Claims 7-8; [0054]);\n- adjacent-channel center spacing 10 mm to 50 mm, with nested examples 10-30 mm\n  and 10-20 mm (Claim 9; [0055]);\n- channel shapes independently rectangular prism, cylindrical, pyramidal, or\n  trapezoidal prism (Claim 10);\n- sloped channels whose width nearest the ORR is 1 percent to 500 percent greater\n  than the width farthest from the ORR (Claim 61; FIG. 5);\n- OER cathode projections of average length 3 mm to 50 mm from a trunk, with\n  nested examples 5-35 mm and 10-30 mm, and width 0.1-20 mm or 0.5-15 mm\n  (Claims 3-4; [0064]-[0065]);\n- greater than 25 percent of channels occupied by OER, with optional greater than\n  40, 50, or 60 percent (Claim 5; [0066]);\n- channel fraction about 0.1 to 0.5, example 0.2 to 0.45, defined as\n  CW/(CW + RW); example ChanFrac 0.4 with w_rib 5 mm ([0247], FIGS. 25-26);\n- iron-to-OER gap 0.1 mm to 5 mm, example 1-4 mm ([0260]).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 Claims 3-10 and 61 and paragraphs [0053]-[0066], [0247], [0260].\nSimulations in FIGS. 19-26 use 6 M KOH at 303 K, initial rib porosity 0.6, and\nloading 4 g Fe/cm2 when comparing planar, channeled, and interdigitated cells.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-016"},"51":{"MID":"da59a72f9ef54c84b7a0e042b69e8712","UID":"IA-SYS-017","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Discrete anodes, discrete OER cathodes, spiral, and pleated layouts","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL support these additional EP4602674A1 independent architectures as\nnamed configurations:\n\n- plurality of anodes separated by channels, electrically communicating, with\n  interdigitated OER (Claim 20; FIG. 12);\n- plurality of OER cathodes interdigitated with the anode array and electrically\n  communicating with each other (Claim 38);\n- iron electrode with channels and a plurality of OER cathodes in the channels\n  (Claim 55);\n- spiral iron electrode and anode current collector with spiral OER at least\n  partially bifilar, ORR facing the spiral axis (Claim 70);\n- pleated iron electrode and pleated OER with channels between pleats (Claim 77;\n  FIG. 17), optionally with OER on both faces of the iron electrode (Claim 80).\n\nDouble-sided cells across a shared current collector (Claim 18; FIGS. 6-8) and\npaired cells sharing an air channel between ORR backsides (Claim 19; FIGS. 9-11)\nSHALL be supported.\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 independent Claims 20, 38, 55, 70, 77 and dependent Claims 18-19.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-017"},"52":{"MID":"169b79247c5a4c548cfd99550bababe9","UID":"IA-SYS-018","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron loading, electrolyte volume fraction, and spine/rib porosity","STATEMENT":"Iron electrode areal loading SHALL be parameterizable from 1 g Fe/cm2 to 7 g Fe/cm2\nrelative to the direction perpendicular to the ORR electrode (EP4602674A1 Claims 16,\n36, 52, 68, 75, 84).\n\nElectrolyte volume fraction in the iron electrode SHALL be parameterizable from\n0.5 to 0.9 based on total electrode volume when fully charged (Claims 17, 37, 53,\n69, 76, 85), measurable in hardware by mercury porosimetry.\n\nSpine portions connecting ribs MAY have electrolyte volume fraction 2-20 vol%\n(example 5-15 vol%) lower than ribs, spine thickness 10-50% of rib thickness, and\nspine porosity at least 5 vol% lower than ribs, or alternatively spine and rib\nporosity within plus or minus 5% ([0059]-[0061]).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 Claims 16-17 and [0059]-[0061]. Higher loading amortizes air-electrode\ncost ([0045]) but planar electrodes lose coulombic efficiency as thickness grows\n([0046]-[0047], FIGS. 23-24).\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-018"},"53":{"MID":"0496822ce2734cb8aaa873817a92947a","UID":"IA-SYS-019","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrode assembly manufacturing sequence","STATEMENT":"A documented assembly scenario SHALL form iron negative material onto an anode\ncurrent collector to create channels, dispose OER into one or more channels, and\nassemble ORR with its first surface facing the iron electrode and second surface\nin contact with air (EP4602674A1 Claim 86).\n\nForming MAY include compressing the iron material onto the current collector\n(Claim 87) and sintering (Claim 88), followed by adding electrolyte (Claim 89).\nDRI pellet beds MAY alternatively be compressed into an anode-current-collector\nassembly using passive mechanical locking or threaded rods (US12308414B2\nFIGS. 92-93).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 Claims 86-89; US12308414B2 FIGS. 92-93. These steps define initial\nporosity, contact resistance, and channel geometry states.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-019"},"54":{"MID":"58ece46b7381433a9a3ad4cc3562eb25","UID":"SEC-SRS-CHEM","TITLE":"Chemistry, electrolyte, and operating conditions","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-CHEM"},"55":{"MID":"db3b9bd9303c457cbf7e1662cc0e7111","STATEMENT":"Discharge of the iron electrode in alkaline electrolyte follows Fe + 2OH- <->\nFe(OH)2 + 2e- then further oxidation to magnetite Fe3O4. EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0043]\ngives theoretical capacities of 960 mAh/g-Fe for the first step and 320 mAh/g-Fe\nfor the second, with net volume increase taken up in anode porosity. US12308414B2\nstates the four-electron air reaction O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -> 4OH- at the ORR triple\nphase boundary. Charge reverses iron reduction and evolves oxygen at the OER.\nHydrogen evolution 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH- competes with recharge.\n","_LINK":"db3b9bd9303c457cbf7e1662cc0e7111"},"56":{"MID":"f15f75b775b84aaeb956a20c12002d2b","UID":"IA-SYS-020","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron-air reaction set","STATEMENT":"The shared chemistry module SHALL implement:\n\n- Fe + 2OH- <-> Fe(OH)2 + 2e- with 960 mAh/g-Fe theoretical first-step capacity\n  (EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0043] Equation 1);\n- subsequent oxidation of iron hydroxide to magnetite Fe3O4 with 320 mAh/g-Fe\n  theoretical second-step capacity (Equation 2);\n- ORR O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -> 4OH- (US12308414B2 detailed description);\n- OER 4OH- -> O2 + 2H2O + 4e- as the independent reverse oxygen couple;\n- HER 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH- as the parasitic charge side reaction\n  (EP4602674A1 [0044], [0047]).\n\nDischarge volume increase SHALL be taken up in anode porosity rather than by\ninventing extra inventory.\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0047]; US12308414B2 air-electrode half-reaction and triple-phase\nboundary discussion. Interdigitated OER reduces ionic path length so charge current\nis less likely to divert to HER at the electrode front ([0050], FIGS. 19-21).\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-020"},"57":{"MID":"fe4a63bc234d4be988303f8b467beda0","UID":"IA-SYS-021","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte composition and pH domain","STATEMENT":"The default liquid electrolyte SHALL be an aqueous alkaline hydroxide solution.\n\nThe model SHALL accept compositions disclosed in the patents, including:\n\n- 6 M KOH (US12308414B2; EP4602674A1 simulation electrolyte at 303 K);\n- 5.5 M KOH + 0.5 M LiOH;\n- 6 M NaOH;\n- 5 M NaOH + 1 M KOH;\n- about 4 M KOH + 2 M NaOH + 0.05 M LiOH, or 4 M NaOH + 2 M KOH + 0.05 M LiOH;\n- high hydroxide at or above 5 M, including 5-6 M, 6-7 M, 7-11 M, 7-10 M,\n  7.5-9.5 M, and 7 M or greater (EP4602674A1 electrolyte section).\n\npH SHALL be modelable as acidic (pH < 4), near-neutral (4 < pH < 10), or alkaline\n(pH > 10), with alkaline pH greater than 10 or greater than 12 as preferred\noperating points (US12308414B2 electrolyte paragraph).\n\nElectrolyte type MAY also be solid oxide, solid polymer, molten salt, non-aqueous,\ngel, or combinations (EP4602674A1 Claims 14-15), but aqueous alkali hydroxide is\nthe verified modeling default.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 liquid electrolyte examples; EP4602674A1 Claims 14-15 and [0231]-[0241].\nHigh hydroxide is disclosed to increase capacity, improve coulombic efficiency, and\ndecrease overpotential.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-021"},"58":{"MID":"e4f280d9cf3f4467a33b885b02a9f679","UID":"IA-SYS-022","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Temperature, pressure, and oxygen partial pressure","STATEMENT":"Unless a scenario states otherwise, property evaluations SHALL use room temperature\n25 C and standard ambient pressure 1 atm as defined in US12308414B2\n(\"room temperature is 25 C\"; \"standard temperature and pressure is 25 C and\n1 atmosphere\"). The term \"about\" SHALL mean a variance of plus or minus 10 percent\nor the larger of instrument error, per that patent.\n\nGas-phase oxygen partial pressure SHALL be parameterizable from 0.01 atm to 100 atm\non discharge and from 0.001 atm to 100 atm on charge, and MAY be raised on\ndischarge and lowered on charge to increase thermodynamic driving force\n(US12308414B2 pressurized-gas paragraph).\n\nEP4602674A1 simulation reference of 6 M KOH at 303 K SHALL be an available\nproperty-model operating point.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 definitions and pO2 operating window; EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-26\nsimulation conditions.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-022"},"59":{"MID":"3a8e77c883674d259d09d7bed571d774","UID":"IA-SYS-023","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Water, carbonation, and hydrogen management","STATEMENT":"The plant SHALL track water inventory changes from ORR/OER, evaporation, and HER.\n\nCarbon dioxide in inlet air SHALL be allowed to carbonate alkaline electrolyte,\nchanging pH and ionic conductivity and precipitating carbonate that can clog pores\n(US12308414B2 CO2 paragraph). A filter/scrubber path MAY circulate electrolyte to\nremove CO2 or carbonate.\n\nFloating inert polymer spheres (polypropylene or HDPE, density below the\nelectrolyte) MAY cover the free electrolyte surface to reduce evaporation and\nalkaline misting.\n\nHER and OER bubbles SHALL have egress paths: vertical channels (EP4602674A1\n[0056]), separator ribbing ([0048]), 0.1-5 mm iron-OER gaps, and enclosure\nventilation (US12308414B2 lid vent 903).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 water, misting-sphere, CO2, and lid-vent disclosures; EP4602674A1\nbubble-egress design of channels and separators.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-023"},"60":{"MID":"99d2c66f20934fd6a6a1e5510b557584","UID":"SEC-SRS-MAT","TITLE":"Materials, collectors, separators, and safety","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-MAT"},"61":{"MID":"bd9f57031ce34fa9859f839f3d0343c3","UID":"IA-SYS-024","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrode and catalyst materials","STATEMENT":"The metal electrode SHALL be selectable as Li, Na, K, Mg, Ca, Si, Al, Zn, or Fe,\nor alloys substantially of those elements including FeAl, FeZn, and FeMg, with\niron or iron alloy as the default LODES anode (US12308414B2 anode paragraph).\n\nAnode physical form SHALL include dense or porous solid, mesh, foam, particles,\nslurry, ink, suspension, paste, and DRI pellet beds.\n\nORR gas-diffusion electrodes SHALL support hydrophobic layers with embedded\nconductive carbon, PTFE microporous layers, and carbon-fiber layers. Example\nloadings include an MPL of about 23 wt% PTFE and 77 wt% carbon black and a first\nportion of about 33 wt% PTFE (US12308414B2 cathode construction).\n\nOER electrodes SHALL support porous metal mesh plus oxygen-evolution catalyst\nincluding nickel, Ni-Fe alloys, manganese oxide, iron, NiOx, nickel oxyhydroxide,\niron oxide, and iron oxyhydroxide (EP4602674A1 Claim 11 and catalyst list).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 anode/cathode materials; EP4602674A1 Claim 11 and OER catalyst\nparagraph. Dual electrodes allow independent material optimization.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-024"},"62":{"MID":"9d861c73ef414a5cb4734f698b6fe525","UID":"IA-SYS-025","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Current collectors, separators, and seals","STATEMENT":"Anode current collectors SHALL support branch collectors parallel to channels plus\na primary collector or bus bar parallel to the ORR first surface, including bus\nbars outside the active-area plane (EP4602674A1 Claims 13, 30-31, 47-48).\n\nBranch collectors SHALL be modelable as highly conductive wires or sheets\n(example stainless steel) inside ribs, or as mesh/cage compressing each rib or\nanode.\n\nORR tab current collectors MAY be placed on both faces of the ORR electrode.\nORR seals MAY sandwich a compliant EPDM gasket between a rigid electrode and a\nNi-coated-steel current collector under compressive load (US12308414B2 FIGS. 4B-4C).\n\nSeparators SHALL block dissolved oxygen and oxygen bubbles from the anode without\nunduly raising ionic resistance, remaining hydrophilic and macroporous\n(US12308414B2 FIG. 4A). Separators MAY be compression frames, porous insulators,\nribbed bubble-egress structures, or porous dielectric coatings (EP4602674A1\n[0048], Claim 2).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 FIGS. 4A-4C; EP4602674A1 Claims 2, 13, 30-31.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-025"},"63":{"MID":"4e111fd506d7464399a8f476fade423b","UID":"IA-SYS-026","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hazardous electrolyte containment and electrical isolation","STATEMENT":"Alkaline electrolyte SHALL be treated as a hazardous liquid requiring primary\ncontainment and secondary containment (US12308414B2 vessel discussion).\n\nOER SHALL remain electrically insulated from the iron electrode and from the ORR\nelectrode except through the intended electrochemical path.\n\nExternal housing surfaces used for can-negative stacking SHALL have sufficient\ninsulation for personnel safety while still allowing intended cell-to-cell\ncontacts (FIG. 11).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 containment and can-negative insulation requirements; EP4602674A1\nOER electrical isolation statement.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-026"},"64":{"MID":"f0c3b90e0e5f43d7b91607ce9841f88f","UID":"SEC-SRS-ODE","TITLE":"Dynamic modeling rules","_LINK":"SEC-SRS-ODE"},"65":{"MID":"368e492ab68549719f9d18ccfc38b824","UID":"IA-SYS-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"ODE-based components","STATEMENT":"Every physical component SHALL expose a dynamic ODE model.\n\nEach component SHALL explicitly define: state vector, state units, initial\nconditions, input variables, output variables, governing differential equations,\nalgebraic constitutive relations, physical parameters, boundary conditions,\nconservation residuals, numerical constraints, and associated StrictDoc\nrequirement IDs.\n\nEvery component SHALL provide a rhs() method returning state derivatives.\n\nThe generic mathematical representation SHALL be:\n\n  dx/dt = f(t, x, inputs, parameters)\n\nWhere required:\n\n  0 = g(t, x, algebraic_variables, inputs, parameters)\n\nAlgebraic variables SHALL be solved consistently during RHS evaluation or through\na supported DAE backend.\n\nAn artificial time constant SHALL NOT be introduced solely to make an instantaneous\nphysical relation appear dynamic.\n\nPassive structures, such as current collectors, SHALL possess actual dynamic states\nwhere justified, such as temperature. Their instantaneous electrical resistance\nSHALL remain an algebraic relation.\n\nPatent-derived algebraic laws SHALL include Ohmic drop, hydraulic orifice and\nequalized stacked-ORR pressure drop, Nernst potential, and Faraday mapping of\ncurrent to reaction rate.\n","COMMENT":"Every DynamicComponent publishes StateSpec metadata and a deterministic\nrhs(t, y, inputs, context). Algebraic electrical resistance is not converted into\na fake lag state.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-003"},"66":{"MID":"316290e65a3c42fb8d8bd04c6eb83366","UID":"IA-SYS-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Shared component models","STATEMENT":"Individual component simulations and the integrated battery SHALL use the same\nimplementation.\n\nThe system SHALL NOT contain separate, simplified physics solely for Jupyter\nexamples.\n\nReduced-order versions MAY be implemented, but SHALL be explicitly identified and\ntested against the corresponding higher-fidelity models.\n\nPatent architecture options (floating versus submerged ORR, planar versus\ninterdigitated OER, bifunctional versus dual cathode) SHALL be selected by\nconfiguration flags on the shared components, not by forked governing equations.\n","COMMENT":"Notebooks import ironair and do not define replacement governing equations for\nproduction physics. Reduced-order models are named with a reduced_order flag or\nclass name and have comparison tests.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-004"},"67":{"MID":"86f5b79733c54b668843b3e64d7fbdee","UID":"IA-SYS-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Simulation fidelity","STATEMENT":"The software SHALL support lumped ODE models, spatially discretized ODE models,\ncoupled differential-algebraic models, reduced-order dynamic models, and fully\nintegrated system simulations.\n\nSpatially distributed PDE formulations SHALL be discretized using finite-volume or\nfinite-difference methods to produce systems of ODEs or DAEs.\n\nThrough-plane ionic gradients in thick iron electrodes and oxygen profiles in GDL\nand stacked ORR columns SHALL be representable by spatial discretization when\nhigh-fidelity mode is selected (EP4602674A1 [0046]-[0047]; US12308414B2 stacked\nORR).\n","COMMENT":"CoupledSystem accepts lumped and spatially discretized components. Spatial\ncomponents document their discretization method and node count.\n","_LINK":"IA-SYS-005"},"68":{"MID":"6ab4a826377945cf8dcf3b60b62666fd","UID":"SEC-SSS1-REQ","TITLE":"Requirements management","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-REQ"},"69":{"MID":"e8059fa44a01438c8a2da5ee7cacdb46","STATEMENT":"This document specifies foundation, chemistry, ODE, notebook, and verification\nrequirements for ironair. Chemical stoichiometry, capacities, electrolyte\ncompositions, and kinetics domains are taken from US12308414B2 and EP4602674A1.\nSoftware tests verify implementation against these statements; they do not\nvalidate the patents experimentally.\n","_LINK":"e8059fa44a01438c8a2da5ee7cacdb46"},"70":{"MID":"0dafab415b0a43fa9bc94ff26bc76a03","UID":"IA-REQ-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"StrictDoc source of truth","STATEMENT":"All requirements SHALL be maintained in StrictDoc .sdoc files.\n\nMarkdown SHALL be used for supplementary explanatory design documents but SHALL NOT\nreplace the authoritative requirements database.\n","COMMENT":"Every IA-* UID exists in reqs/**/*.sdoc. CI fails if a source @relation UID is missing\nfrom the StrictDoc database.\n","_LINK":"IA-REQ-001"},"71":{"MID":"ecdfa6bcf7fc4ebc81b101fa3e8767e3","UID":"IA-REQ-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Requirement identifiers","STATEMENT":"Each requirement SHALL possess a permanent unique ID.\n\nRequired prefixes: IA-SYS, IA-REQ, IA-CON, IA-CHM, IA-FE, IA-HER, IA-ORR, IA-OER,\nIA-GDL, IA-ELY, IA-SEP, IA-COL, IA-CEL, IA-THM, IA-AIR, IA-FLD, IA-HEX, IA-STK,\nIA-MOD, IA-ENC, IA-DC, IA-CNV, IA-INV, IA-TRF, IA-GRD, IA-CTL, IA-EST, IA-DEG,\nIA-FLT, IA-ODE, IA-NBK, IA-PLT, IA-TST, IA-DOC.\n\nPatent-mapped system requirements live under IA-SYS in DOC-SRS001 (US12308414B2\nClaims 1-18 and EP4602674A1 Claims 1-89). Component prefixes SHALL be used for\nthe corresponding physical models of those claims.\n","COMMENT":"UID uniqueness is verified by tests. All required prefixes appear in the requirements tree.\n","_LINK":"IA-REQ-002"},"72":{"MID":"abb4875367f64f4d81bcc0869ec781ca","UID":"IA-REQ-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Function-level traceability","STATEMENT":"Every public physics, control, solver, plotting, and scenario function SHALL contain a\nStrictDoc @relation marker.\n\nA function implementing several requirements SHALL contain separate relation markers\nfor every applicable requirement.\n\nInternal helper functions SHALL be traceable to the applicable implementation requirements.\n\nThe traceability system SHALL identify Requirement -> Python Function -> Test -> Notebook Scenario.\n","COMMENT":"CI greps @relation(UID, scope=function) in src/ironair and maps each implementation\nrequirement to at least one function.\n","_LINK":"IA-REQ-003"},"73":{"MID":"f631905e98dd432d9e3b8eb86055c58d","UID":"IA-REQ-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Bidirectional traceability","STATEMENT":"CI SHALL verify that every implementation requirement has an associated function,\nevery required function exists, every required function is covered by tests,\nevery documented scenario has a notebook, every required notebook executes successfully,\nall source-level requirement references resolve to valid StrictDoc UIDs, and every\nrequirements-to-source reference identifies an existing Python source element.\n","COMMENT":"tests/test_requirements_traceability.py fails the build on missing UIDs, missing\nfunctions, missing tests, or dangling file relations.\n","_LINK":"IA-REQ-004"},"74":{"MID":"668b8cb198a7498f8c9abf90b5b356bb","UID":"IA-REQ-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Requirement verification","STATEMENT":"Every physical requirement SHALL specify acceptance criteria that can be objectively tested.\n\nCompliance SHALL be verified through unit tests, integration tests, conservation checks,\nnumerical convergence tests, and documented model validation.\n\nPassing software tests SHALL NOT be represented as physical validation against experimental hardware.\n","COMMENT":"Physical requirements include ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA. Validation status is tracked separately\nfrom software verification in ironair.validation. Patent claims are traced by UID\nand source citation, not by copying claim text as a test oracle.\n","_LINK":"IA-REQ-005"},"75":{"MID":"1f80555d32c24153a25b9de09de97d31","UID":"SEC-SSS1-CON","TITLE":"Constants, parameters, and property models","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-CON"},"76":{"MID":"6004170d48844d778d6d715b786ba385","STATEMENT":"US12308414B2 defines room temperature as 25 C and standard temperature and\npressure as 25 C and 1 atm, with \"about\" meaning plus or minus 10 percent or\ninstrument error, whichever is larger. EP4602674A1 simulations use 6 M KOH at\n303 K. Property models SHALL cover those reference states.\n","_LINK":"6004170d48844d778d6d715b786ba385"},"77":{"MID":"f022b7cacad64078b659ba2ff7f6a949","UID":"IA-CON-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constants module","STATEMENT":"Implement src/ironair/constants.py providing F_C_MOL, R_J_MOL_K, T_REF_K, P_REF_PA,\nM_FE_KG_MOL, M_O2_KG_MOL, M_H2O_KG_MOL, and M_KOH_KG_MOL.\n\nT_REF_K SHALL default to 298.15 K (25 C) and P_REF_PA to 101325 Pa (1 atm) to\nmatch US12308414B2 standard ambient temperature and pressure. A 303 K property\nset SHALL be available for EP4602674A1 simulation conditions.\n\nFundamental constants SHALL be immutable.\n","COMMENT":"from ironair import constants succeeds. Named constants match CODATA/IUPAC values\nwithin documented precision. Assignment to module constants raises an error or is\nrejected by typing/runtime freeze.\n","_LINK":"IA-CON-001"},"78":{"MID":"ca9f7b57e21f4d958ec1ad64c073147f","UID":"IA-CON-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Parameter definitions","STATEMENT":"Every component SHALL use explicit, typed parameter objects.\n\nParameters SHALL include name, value, SI unit, physical meaning, valid range,\nsource classification, literature reference when available, uncertainty when available,\nand calibration status.\n\nParameter categories SHALL distinguish fundamental constant, literature value,\nexperimental measurement, engineering design assumption, calibrated coefficient,\nand unverified placeholder.\n\nPatent-disclosed values (electrolyte molarity, channel lengths, iron loading,\npO2 windows, theoretical mAh/g) SHALL be tagged as literature value with the\npatent number and claim or paragraph in the literature-reference field. They\nSHALL NOT be tagged as experimental measurement unless an independent hardware\ncampaign is recorded.\n","COMMENT":"PhysicalParameter dataclass enforces required fields and rejects out-of-range values.\nUnverified placeholders are queryable via calibration_status.\n","_LINK":"IA-CON-002"},"79":{"MID":"c3282ce155994c1380e1f4d23aff1299","UID":"IA-CON-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Property models","STATEMENT":"Implement concentration- and temperature-dependent properties for KOH conductivity,\nKOH density, KOH viscosity, KOH heat capacity, KOH water activity, dissolved oxygen\nsolubility, oxygen diffusivity, gas-phase diffusivity, iron conductivity, thermal\nconductivity, reaction rate constants, and electrochemical exchange-current densities.\n\nProperty models SHALL enforce their valid domains.\n\nKOH molarity domain SHALL include the patented compositions 5 M to 11 M hydroxide,\nincluding 6 M KOH, 5.5 M KOH + 0.5 M LiOH, 6 M NaOH, 5 M NaOH + 1 M KOH, and\n7 M or greater hydroxide (US12308414B2 electrolyte examples; EP4602674A1\n[0231]-[0241]).\n","COMMENT":"Property functions raise a domain error outside documented T and concentration ranges\nand return finite SI values inside the range.\n","_LINK":"IA-CON-003"},"80":{"MID":"95c1782d52d84e13ab402faa07dc9570","UID":"SEC-SSS1-CHM","TITLE":"Shared iron-air chemistry (US12308414B2 and EP4602674A1)","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-CHM"},"81":{"MID":"e42972c89990463688c8718ebd6fea04","STATEMENT":"EP4602674A1 Equations 1-2 and US12308414B2 air-electrode half-reactions are the\nstoichiometric source for ironair.chemistry. Elemental conservation is a software\nrequirement; the patents supply the reaction identities and theoretical capacities.\n","_LINK":"e42972c89990463688c8718ebd6fea04"},"82":{"MID":"fec1a95b3d804281a77bab0e3a68342d","UID":"IA-CHM-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stoichiometric reaction network","STATEMENT":"Implement a common chemical reaction network.\n\nMathematical model:\n\n  dn/dt = S @ r + B @ q\n\nWhere S is the chemical stoichiometric matrix, r is the reaction-rate vector,\nB maps boundary flows to species inventories, and q is the boundary-flow vector.\n\nThe reaction network SHALL enforce elemental conservation.\n","COMMENT":"For every reaction column, elemental composition @ S[:, j] is numerically zero.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-001"},"83":{"MID":"4a9a4d57db3c427bafa0eca59070b897","UID":"IA-CHM-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron conversion","STATEMENT":"Implement Fe + 2OH- <-> Fe(OH)2 + 2e- as EP4602674A1 [0042] Equation 1.\n\nCalculate reaction rates from electrode kinetics and Faraday's law.\n\nTheoretical first-step capacity SHALL be 960 mAh per gram of metallic Fe\n(EP4602674A1 [0043]). Net volume increase on discharge SHALL be accommodated in\nanode porosity rather than by creating unphysical inventory.\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0043]. US12308414B2 identifies Fe and Fe-alloys (FeAl, FeZn,\nFeMg) as metal-electrode options; iron is the default LODES anode.\n","COMMENT":"Two electrons per iron atom. Faraday mapping r = i * A / (n * F) holds within solver tolerance.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-002"},"84":{"MID":"af0a93f7ae744d28a040a7588f1b1709","UID":"IA-CHM-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Magnetite formation","STATEMENT":"Implement configurable conversion between iron hydroxide and magnetite, including\nelectron transfer and correct stoichiometry.\n\nThe second discharge step SHALL correspond to further oxidation of Fe(OH)2 to\nFe3O4 with theoretical capacity 320 mAh per gram of Fe (EP4602674A1 [0043]\nEquation 2). Charge SHALL reduce higher-valence iron species toward Fe(0)\n(EP4602674A1 [0049]).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0042]-[0043], [0049]. Magnetite is the example higher-valence product\non discharge through the dual-electrode load path.\n","COMMENT":"Default electrochemical path conserves Fe, O, H, and charge. A Schikorr chemical path\nmay be enabled without inventing extra electrons.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-003"},"85":{"MID":"5a4558798edb419fbabff2550b00f0df","UID":"IA-CHM-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen reduction reaction","STATEMENT":"Implement O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -> 4OH- as the ORR discharge reaction of\nUS12308414B2 (four-electron oxygen reduction at the gas-solid-electrolyte\ntriple-phase boundary).\n\nORR SHALL consume gaseous oxygen typically sourced from air and SHALL require\ntriple-phase contact among O2, an electronically conductive solid, and\nelectrolyte.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 detailed description of cathode 120 / GDE operation. EP4602674A1\nFIG. 1 ORR electrode 104 is the discharge positive.\n","COMMENT":"Four electrons per O2. OH- production matches Faraday's law.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-004"},"86":{"MID":"73252fd7049f4c71a91a5584fa5cfb9b","UID":"IA-CHM-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen evolution reaction","STATEMENT":"Implement 4OH- -> O2 + 2H2O + 4e- as the OER charge reaction on a dedicated\nOER electrode (US12308414B2 dual cathode; EP4602674A1 [0048]-[0049]).\n\nOER kinetics, catalyst state, and bubble generation SHALL be independent of ORR\nso that high OER potentials do not oxidize the ORR electrode when the electrodes\nare electrically separated (US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B).\n","RATIONALE":"Dual-electrode configuration in both patents. EP4602674A1 Claim 11: OER as porous\nmetal mesh plus oxygen-evolution catalyst (Ni, Ni-Fe, Mn oxide, Fe, NiOx,\nNiOx(OH)y, FeOx, FeOx(OH)y).\n","COMMENT":"OER is the reverse four-electron oxygen couple of ORR with independent kinetics.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-005"},"87":{"MID":"02b5181061084914a2bc668d5b296e93","UID":"IA-CHM-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hydrogen evolution reaction","STATEMENT":"Implement 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH- as the parasitic hydrogen evolution reaction\nthat competes with iron recharge (EP4602674A1 [0044], [0047]).\n\nHER SHALL be favored at the electrode front when ionic path length to unreacted\noxidized iron at the back is large. Channeled and interdigitated geometries\nSHALL reduce that path length and SHALL be able to increase coulombic efficiency\nrelative to planar electrodes (EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-21).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0044]-[0050] and FIGS. 19-26. Water as HER reactant can be replenished\nwhile iron hydroxide at the back remains transport-limited.\n","COMMENT":"Two electrons per H2. Rate increases with more negative iron overpotential.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-006"},"88":{"MID":"5e3d3e29b67e43f3a5e6c1e772df2063","UID":"IA-CHM-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Butler-Volmer kinetics","STATEMENT":"Implement nonlinear electrode kinetics with exchange-current density, transfer coefficients,\nlocal overpotential, temperature, species activities, electrode surface area, and\nreaction availability.\n\nExchange-current densities SHALL be temperature- and concentration-dependent\nwithin the patented alkaline domain. Interdigitated OER SHALL be allowed to\nlower effective ionic overpotential relative to planar OER at the same loading\n(EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-24).\n","COMMENT":"Zero overpotential yields zero net Faradaic current at unit activities. Current is\nodd in overpotential for symmetric coefficients.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-007"},"89":{"MID":"e3fe5b2fe7fe40ac9dd70e8fe58689d6","UID":"IA-CHM-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Nernst potentials","STATEMENT":"Calculate equilibrium potentials using thermodynamically consistent activities and\nstandard electrode-potential references.\n","COMMENT":"Nernst potentials shift (RT/nF) ln(activity quotient) relative to documented E0.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-008"},"90":{"MID":"696c510d9ea34923a678f34050242965","UID":"IA-CHM-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reaction thermodynamics","STATEMENT":"Calculate Gibbs free energy, reaction enthalpy, reaction entropy, equilibrium cell\npotential, reversible reaction heat, and irreversible reaction heat.\n","COMMENT":"E_eq = -DeltaG / (n F). Irreversible heat is eta * i * A. Reversible heat uses T * DeltaS.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-009"},"91":{"MID":"acbb2bc1c4e94ca49a5e5a15df9510ef","UID":"IA-CHM-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Chemistry conservation","STATEMENT":"The central chemistry module SHALL check iron atom conservation, oxygen atom conservation,\nhydrogen atom conservation, potassium conservation, carbon conservation, charge balance,\nelectron transfer, and reaction stoichiometry.\n","COMMENT":"Elemental residual norms are below 1e-12 relative to characteristic inventory for\nclosed-system reaction extents.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-010"},"92":{"MID":"e7f6929f0220494f8b3f797a662144f2","UID":"IA-CHM-011","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Coulombic efficiency versus electrode architecture","STATEMENT":"The chemistry-to-electrode coupling SHALL report instantaneous coulombic charge\nefficiency as the fraction of charge current stored in intended iron reduction\nproducts rather than HER (EP4602674A1 [0044], FIGS. 19, 21, 23, 25).\n\nPlanar, channeled, and interdigitated OER/iron layouts SHALL be comparable on\nthe same ParameterSet. Interdigitated layouts SHALL be able to show higher\ncoulombic efficiency and lower sensitivity of efficiency to areal loading than\nplanar layouts, matching the qualitative simulation results of EP4602674A1\nFIGS. 19-24 at 6 M KOH, 303 K, initial rib porosity 0.6, example ChanFrac 0.4,\nw_rib 5 mm, and example loading 4 g Fe/cm2.\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0261]-[0280] and FIGS. 19-27. FIG. 27 experimental interdigitated\ncell showed an extended upper discharge voltage plateau versus planar control\nat matched loading, temperature, current, electrolyte, and ORR.\n","_LINK":"IA-CHM-011"},"93":{"MID":"527f5832f5664e83a157a2fccb0b71b8","UID":"SEC-SSS1-ODE","TITLE":"ODE protocol, solvers, and multiscale execution","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-ODE"},"94":{"MID":"494071bf097847f7a92b93e75bb5f7a2","UID":"IA-ODE-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Common component protocol","STATEMENT":"Every dynamic component SHALL implement rhs(self, t, y, inputs, context).\n\nThe RHS SHALL be deterministic for a fixed state, time, input, and parameter configuration.\n\nIt SHALL NOT modify component state through hidden side effects.\n","COMMENT":"Repeated rhs evaluations with identical arguments return identical arrays. Component\nparameter objects are unchanged after rhs.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-001"},"95":{"MID":"8d1ae819224d492cb7e81d2eb51b2d14","UID":"IA-ODE-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"State definitions","STATEMENT":"Each component SHALL publish named state metadata and a validated state vector layout.\n\nState arrays SHALL be NumPy arrays.\n","COMMENT":"y0 size matches len(state_specs). Units and names are unique per component.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-002"},"96":{"MID":"2cd7db45ab5848848059f51a003e0485","UID":"IA-ODE-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Coupled solver","STATEMENT":"Implement ironair.simulation.CoupledSystem.\n\nThe solver SHALL assemble component states into one global state vector, maintain a\nstate-index map, resolve shared inputs and algebraic couplings, evaluate the coupled\nRHS, pass the assembled system to SciPy, detect numerical failures, and return state\ntrajectories and diagnostics.\n","COMMENT":"simulate() returns a SimulationResult with t, y, names, and solver diagnostics.\nFailure of solve_ivp is reported rather than silently truncated.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-003"},"97":{"MID":"b590e8b661d84a04a6856af70a6f4017","UID":"IA-ODE-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stiff solvers","STATEMENT":"The default integrated system SHALL use a stiff-capable solver, normally SciPy BDF or Radau.\n\nNonstiff component demonstrations MAY use RK45 or DOP853.\n","COMMENT":"CoupledSystem default method is BDF or Radau.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-004"},"98":{"MID":"35df5d83d3624f41a1227b12eedf56ab","UID":"IA-ODE-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Numerical robustness","STATEMENT":"The simulation SHALL include state-specific absolute tolerances, relative tolerances,\nJacobian sparsity, event detection, hybrid mode transitions, nonnegative inventory\nchecks, solver diagnostics, and energy and mass conservation residuals.\n","COMMENT":"SimulationResult exposes atol vector, events, conservation residuals, and solver status.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-005"},"99":{"MID":"2cb895a55cc345df935e0ab182deca2d","UID":"IA-ODE-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Multiscale execution","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL support millisecond, second, minute, hour, and multi-day simulation windows.\n\nDetailed electrochemical dynamics and long-duration grid dispatch SHALL be able to use\ndifferent model fidelity settings while sharing the same physical parameter definitions.\n","COMMENT":"The same ParameterSet can be passed to detailed and reduced-order components.\nt_span may be [0, 1e-3] or [0, 7*86400] without changing parameter objects.\nLODES windows of 8 h, 100 h, 300 h, week-scale, and seasonal duration from\nUS12308414B2 SHALL use the same ParameterSet with documented fidelity flags.\n","_LINK":"IA-ODE-006"},"100":{"MID":"54f25e4ca2d84512b974eaa7bad86ed7","UID":"SEC-SSS1-NBK","TITLE":"Notebooks and plotting","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-NBK"},"101":{"MID":"c93fdfe045a84a22ae3c5e0837f4ae6e","UID":"IA-NBK-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Notebook development environment","STATEMENT":"All notebooks SHALL be located in notebooks/.\n\nAll notebooks SHALL import numerical models from the installed ironair package.\n\nNo notebook SHALL contain a separate replacement implementation of a battery physics model.\n","COMMENT":"Notebook AST/source scan finds no duplicate rhs or stoichiometric matrix definitions.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-001"},"102":{"MID":"c0d4d113aca84fd58b6d48391b19da6f","UID":"IA-NBK-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Seaborn visualization","STATEMENT":"All scenario notebooks SHALL use seaborn for the principal plots.\n\nMatplotlib MAY be used for figure management, multi-axis plots, annotations, and\nspecialized graphics.\n\nPlots SHALL include title, axis labels, physical units, legend where necessary,\nscenario identifier, and physical operating assumptions.\n","COMMENT":"Principal plotting helpers call seaborn and require title, xlabel, ylabel, and scenario_id.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-002"},"103":{"MID":"32034c2bca5240a4af817e86dc751ee2","UID":"IA-NBK-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Shared plotting API","STATEMENT":"Implement reusable plotting functions under ironair.plotting.\n\nPlotting functions SHALL accept structured simulation results or pandas DataFrames.\n","COMMENT":"from ironair import plotting succeeds and plot_states accepts SimulationResult or DataFrame.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-003"},"104":{"MID":"695e95106c8a40028190cf3ab34f8b59","UID":"IA-NBK-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Chemical model demonstrations","STATEMENT":"Immediately after implementing the baseline chemistry, create notebooks demonstrating\niron oxidation, iron reduction, iron phase inventories, Butler-Volmer kinetics,\nNernst potential, ORR kinetics, OER kinetics, hydrogen evolution, oxygen limitation,\nKOH conductivity, electrolyte concentration, reaction heat, and mass and charge conservation.\n\nEach notebook SHALL call the actual associated Python component.\n","COMMENT":"Required chemistry notebooks exist under notebooks/chemistry and import ironair.chemistry.\nNotebooks SHALL cite US12308414B2 and EP4602674A1 for reaction identities and\nshall not present software plots as hardware validation of those patents.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-004"},"105":{"MID":"b4c3a72c685b4ca89cdd2482c23a8c73","UID":"IA-NBK-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Component demonstrations","STATEMENT":"Every component SHALL have a notebook or explicitly designated notebook section containing\ncomponent configuration, initial conditions, input stimulus, simulation execution,\ntime-domain plots, parameter sensitivity, conservation or physical validation results,\nand discussion of observed behavior.\n","COMMENT":"tests/test_notebook_inventory.py maps each component class to a notebook path.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-005"},"106":{"MID":"887b3b32e1634028accf8496cf3c281d","UID":"IA-NBK-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Full-system scenario demonstrations","STATEMENT":"The notebook suite SHALL cover all required operating scenarios described in Section 9.\n","COMMENT":"Every IA-NBK-007 through IA-NBK-016 scenario ID has a notebook and a Python scenario function.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-006"},"107":{"MID":"f1049ede441a49b9ae75939cc438cab8","UID":"IA-PLT-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Plot styling contract","STATEMENT":"Shared plotting functions SHALL apply a consistent seaborn theme and include physical units\non labeled axes.\n","COMMENT":"plotting helpers reject empty axis labels.\n","_LINK":"IA-PLT-001"},"108":{"MID":"75b95de937814105a35e69e5b23db444","UID":"SEC-SSS1-TST","TITLE":"Verification, conservation, and reproducibility","_LINK":"SEC-SSS1-TST"},"109":{"MID":"5cf28732c389411ba4b0230c8618889e","UID":"IA-TST-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Elemental conservation","STATEMENT":"The stoichiometric network SHALL conserve chemical elements for every reaction.\n","COMMENT":"max(abs(element_matrix @ S)) < 1e-12.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-001"},"110":{"MID":"c0541cad7a784362bd3f01aa6dad8da3","UID":"IA-TST-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge conservation","STATEMENT":"Electrode currents, capacitive currents, and reaction rates SHALL obey conservation of charge.\n","COMMENT":"Sum of Faradaic, capacitive, and terminal currents is below 1e-9 A relative residual.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-002"},"111":{"MID":"25c9be6e10a04da793433abb2846dfad","UID":"IA-TST-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Energy conservation","STATEMENT":"The coupled system SHALL account for electrical, chemical, thermal, and material-flow energy.\n","COMMENT":"Closed-system energy residual is reported and below documented tolerance for the adiabatic cell test.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-003"},"112":{"MID":"752429fb68454eac9ae320e30fc50646","UID":"IA-TST-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Physical state validity","STATEMENT":"The simulation SHALL reject or explicitly report invalid physical states including negative\nspecies inventory, nonphysical porosity, invalid electrolyte volume, nonphysical absolute\ntemperature, invalid pressure, impossible oxygen consumption, and nonphysical cell voltage\nor current conditions.\n","COMMENT":"ironair.exceptions.InvalidPhysicalState is raised or recorded on invalid states.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-004"},"113":{"MID":"29b2b687d29840628add6cfc7177bf9f","UID":"IA-TST-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Numerical convergence","STATEMENT":"The system SHALL include solver tolerance refinement tests and, for spatial models,\nmesh refinement tests.\n","COMMENT":"Halving rtol reduces trajectory error against a tight-tolerance reference.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-005"},"114":{"MID":"dc82827fcc6442aeade3b8aae148ed82","UID":"IA-TST-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Model validation tracking","STATEMENT":"Software verification SHALL be distinguished from model validation.\n\nExperimental calibration and independent physical validation SHALL be separately tracked.\n","COMMENT":"Parameter source_class and validation.ValidationRecord never treat unit-test pass as\nhardware validation.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-006"},"115":{"MID":"e341c42d90c4444793ea310996b78748","UID":"IA-TST-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Automated unit tests","STATEMENT":"Every exported physical calculation and dynamic component SHALL have automated pytest coverage.\n","COMMENT":"pytest coverage for src/ironair is at least 80 percent.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-007"},"116":{"MID":"cb5227c460b84f1985194dc198fd8245","UID":"IA-TST-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Component integration tests","STATEMENT":"Every coupled component interface SHALL have integration tests verifying dimensions,\nunits, and conservation.\n","COMMENT":"CoupledSystem with a cell plus thermal node conserves charge and reports unit-consistent outputs.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-008"},"117":{"MID":"1ded998319ec40229beebe89078f5c7c","UID":"IA-TST-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Notebook execution tests","STATEMENT":"Every mandatory notebook SHALL execute from a clean kernel without manual intervention.\n","COMMENT":"pytest marker notebook executes required notebooks via nbclient.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-009"},"118":{"MID":"86c6f2b6daea4610bd802a03b26b541b","UID":"IA-TST-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Requirements coverage gate","STATEMENT":"CI SHALL fail when a required UID is missing, a requirement has no implementation function,\na required function is missing, a required equation is not tested, a notebook references\nan unavailable API, a mandatory notebook fails execution, or conservation acceptance\ncriteria fail.\n","COMMENT":"The traceability test enumerates these failure modes.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-010"},"119":{"MID":"fa9ef3df3e0d41aab96930c126bd5963","UID":"IA-TST-011","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reproducibility","STATEMENT":"The simulation SHALL support deterministic parameter sets, recorded software versions,\nand fixed random seeds.\n","COMMENT":"SimulationResult.metadata includes ironair version and numpy seed. Sensor noise is\nseed-controlled.\n","_LINK":"IA-TST-011"},"120":{"MID":"f739e9aeda114255ac87eae9fffdc6e7","UID":"IA-DOC-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Package documentation","STATEMENT":"The project SHALL publish architecture, API documentation, model limitations,\nrequirements coverage, and executable notebook demonstrations.\n","COMMENT":"README documents install, architecture, limitations, and that passing tests are not\nhardware validation of US12308414B2 or EP4602674A1.\n","_LINK":"IA-DOC-001"},"121":{"MID":"f4271e2b4c064ae1acff2217a9b89c4d","UID":"SEC-SSS2-ELECTRODES","TITLE":"Iron electrode, hydrogen evolution, and dual air electrodes","_LINK":"SEC-SSS2-ELECTRODES"},"122":{"MID":"8d45eae71c3f426d953eac2587b037fc","STATEMENT":"Each DynamicComponent in this document is the software image of a patented plant\npart. Iron, ORR, OER, electrolyte, separator, collector, cell, stack, module,\nand enclosure geometries are taken from US12308414B2 (Claims 1-18 and FIGS. 1-93)\nand EP4602674A1 (Claims 1-89 and FIGS. 1-27). Balance-of-plant electrical and\nthermal components remain required to close the LODES plant of US12308414B2\nFIGS. 94-102.\n","_LINK":"8d45eae71c3f426d953eac2587b037fc"},"123":{"MID":"cd0d1bcf832c4e07aa7ba705fa0582f2","UID":"IA-FE-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron electrode ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.iron_electrode SHALL provide class IronElectrode.\n\nThe component SHALL dynamically model metallic iron, Fe(OH)2, Fe3O4, active surface\narea, passivation thickness, porosity, electrode temperature, and irreversible\nactive-material loss.\n\nThe RHS SHALL calculate iron phase derivatives from the shared reaction network.\n\nThe iron electrode SHALL support DRI pellet beds (US12308414B2 Claim 18), porous\nparticle assemblies, and channeled rib-and-spine electrodes (EP4602674A1 Claim 1)\nwith areal loading 1-7 g Fe/cm2 and electrolyte volume fraction 0.5-0.9 when\nfully charged (EP4602674A1 Claim 16-17).\n\nChannel length, width, spacing, shape, slope, channel fraction, and spine/rib\nporosity SHALL match IA-SYS-016 and IA-SYS-018. Position relative to ORR and OER\nSHALL be changeable (US12308414B2 Claim 13).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claim 18 and FIG. 3 packed DRI bed; EP4602674A1 Claims 1, 16-17 and\n[0051]-[0061]. Thick electrodes need channels to keep HER from dominating charge.\n","COMMENT":"Discharge of Fe increases Fe(OH)2 using IA-CHM-002 stoichiometry. Temperature is a\ndynamic state. Passivation thickness is nonnegative.\n","_LINK":"IA-FE-001"},"124":{"MID":"7c6b8ba4182a45eea9a50526c18deac5","UID":"IA-HER-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hydrogen evolution ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.hydrogen SHALL provide class HydrogenEvolution.\n\nStates SHALL include hydrogen inventory and applicable interfacial or gas-transfer dynamics.\n\nThe component SHALL couple hydrogen generation to charging current and electrode potential.\n\nVertical channels SHALL provide a bubble-egress path for HER (EP4602674A1 [0056]).\nCoulombic efficiency SHALL decrease when ionic path length to the back of a thick\niron electrode exceeds the resistance of front-surface HER (EP4602674A1 [0047]).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 [0044]-[0047], [0056]. Enclosure ventilation and lid venting in\nUS12308414B2 remove evolved hydrogen from the vessel headspace.\n","COMMENT":"H2 inventory increases under negative iron overpotential. Open-circuit HER is smaller\nthan strong cathodic charging HER.\n","_LINK":"IA-HER-001"},"125":{"MID":"5d1e7929b29d4d9d9d4c5efbe2377cc5","UID":"IA-ORR-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen reduction ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.oxygen_reduction SHALL provide class OxygenReduction.\n\nStates SHALL include interfacial charge storage and oxygen availability where applicable.\n\nThe component SHALL calculate nonlinear ORR kinetics and oxygen consumption.\n\nORR SHALL be configurable as floating, vertical natural-air-breathing, inverse\nair-breathing, tubular, stacked submerged sections with depth-equalized pressure\ndrop, wavy/rippled, or bifunctional, per IA-SYS-009 and IA-SYS-012\n(US12308414B2 Claims 1, 4, 7-12).\n\nOne ORR face SHALL face electrolyte or channels and the opposing face SHALL\ncontact air (EP4602674A1 Claim 1). Hydrophobic GDE layers with PTFE/carbon MPL\nexamples from US12308414B2 SHALL be parameterizable.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 1, 4, 7-12 and cathode construction; EP4602674A1 Claim 1\nair-facing second surface.\n","COMMENT":"ORR current is negative (cathodic) for eta < 0 versus the ORR Nernst potential.\nOxygen consumption matches Faraday's law for four-electron ORR.\n","_LINK":"IA-ORR-001"},"126":{"MID":"edce1979e5ec48af83037d78d67a607b","UID":"IA-OER-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen evolution ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.oxygen_evolution SHALL provide class OxygenEvolution.\n\nThe component SHALL calculate oxygen evolution, catalyst activity, and applicable\ndynamic electrode states.\n\nOER SHALL support planar, submerged, interdigitated trunk-and-projection,\ncorrugated, serpentine, discrete cathode arrays, spiral bifilar, and pleated\nlayouts (EP4602674A1 Claims 1, 3, 12, 32, 38, 70, 77).\n\nDefault OER construction SHALL be porous metal mesh plus oxygen-evolution\ncatalyst (Claim 11). OER SHALL sit closer to the iron electrode than ORR during\ncharge and SHALL be electrically isolatable from ORR (US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 Claims 1, 3, 11-12, 38, 70, 77; US12308414B2 dual-cathode toggle.\n","COMMENT":"OER current is positive (anodic) for eta > 0 versus the OER Nernst potential.\n","_LINK":"IA-OER-001"},"127":{"MID":"7eb03923f16f4dc8acf5a6e5b2b732c5","UID":"IA-GDL-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Gas diffusion ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.gas_diffusion SHALL provide class GasDiffusionLayer.\n\nThe component SHALL calculate transient oxygen concentration profiles, effective\ndiffusion, pore saturation, flooding, and gas/liquid transport.\n\nHydraulic head on vertical ORR SHALL be able to drive liquid migration and\nflooding; OER bubbles trapped at a horizontal air electrode SHALL be able to\ndry the triple-phase boundary (US12308414B2 vertical versus horizontal\narchitecture discussion, FIGS. 8-9B). Stacked submerged ORR SHALL capture\ncascading bubbles with baffles, lips, or increasing pocket thickness\n(FIGS. 25A-36B, Claims 1 and 14-15).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 flooding, dry-out, bubble-trap, and stacked-core air utilization.\npO2 window 0.01-100 atm discharge and 0.001-100 atm charge.\n","COMMENT":"A step decrease in boundary O2 mole fraction produces a delayed interior O2 decline.\nFlooding (saturation -> 1) reduces effective O2 diffusivity.\n","_LINK":"IA-GDL-001"},"128":{"MID":"ddbe5c02863549d78304f37478758153","UID":"IA-ELY-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.electrolyte SHALL provide class Electrolyte.\n\nStates SHALL include water inventory, dissolved species, electrolyte temperature,\ncarbonation, and spatial concentration distributions where enabled.\n\nThe model SHALL include concentration-dependent KOH physical properties.\n\nDefault composition SHALL be 6 M aqueous KOH. Alternate patented mixtures from\nIA-SYS-021 SHALL be selectable. Carbonation from CO2-bearing air SHALL consume\nOH- and MAY precipitate solids that clog pores (US12308414B2). High hydroxide\n(7 M or greater) SHALL be allowed as a capacity and coulombic-efficiency lever\n(EP4602674A1).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 electrolyte examples and CO2 discussion; EP4602674A1 Claims 14-15\nand high-hydroxide paragraph.\n","COMMENT":"Conductivity is computed from instantaneous KOH concentration and temperature via\nironair.properties. Carbonate accumulation consumes OH-.\n","_LINK":"IA-ELY-001"},"129":{"MID":"06f0c062581343a999d0448cd0873276","UID":"IA-SEP-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Separator ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.separator SHALL provide class Separator.\n\nThe model SHALL represent transient species transport, electrolyte saturation where\napplicable, degradation, and separator temperature.\n\nIonic resistance SHALL be calculated from the instantaneous physical state.\n\nThe separator SHALL be able to block dissolved oxygen and oxygen bubbles from\nreaching the iron electrode without a substantial ionic-resistance penalty, while\nremaining hydrophilic and macroporous (US12308414B2 FIG. 4A). It MAY be a\ncompression frame, porous insulator, ribbed bubble-egress structure, or porous\ndielectric coating on iron and/or OER (EP4602674A1 [0048], Claim 2). Wrapped\nstacked-core separators MAY use 1 micrometer to 1 centimeter pores\n(US12308414B2 FIG. 36A).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 oxygen-blocking separator; EP4602674A1 Claim 2 and [0048].\n","COMMENT":"R_ionic is algebraic in conductivity, thickness, area, and saturation. Temperature\nis dynamic.\n","_LINK":"IA-SEP-001"},"130":{"MID":"3fca5d93cfce43fa8c3264c9825bc3b6","UID":"IA-COL-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Current collector ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.current_collector SHALL provide class CurrentCollector.\n\nThe model SHALL include thermal dynamics and configurable long-term contact or\nresistance degradation.\n\nOhmic voltage loss SHALL be calculated algebraically.\n\nCollectors SHALL include anode branch-plus-primary layouts, ORR dual-face tabs,\nNi-coated-steel ORR collectors with EPDM gasket compression\n(US12308414B2 FIGS. 4B-4C), stainless-steel wires/sheets or mesh cages in iron\nribs (EP4602674A1), and can-negative housings (US12308414B2 Claim 5).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 5 and 17 and FIGS. 4B-4C; EP4602674A1 Claims 13, 30-31.\n","COMMENT":"No fake lag state for resistance. V_ohm = I * R(T, degradation_state).\n","_LINK":"IA-COL-001"},"131":{"MID":"8cf711d077284ab9a54d1c64311cd44e","UID":"IA-CEL-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Complete cell ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.cell SHALL provide class IronAirCell.\n\nThe cell SHALL couple iron electrode, air electrode, hydrogen evolution, electrolyte,\nseparator, gas diffusion, current collectors, and thermal dynamics.\n\nThe cell SHALL calculate instantaneous terminal voltage, current, power, SOC, and\navailable capacity.\n\nThe component SHALL solve coupled nonlinear electrochemical and electrical constraints.\n\nCharge SHALL close iron to OER to power source; discharge SHALL close iron to ORR\nto load (EP4602674A1 [0049]). Independent charge and discharge length scales\nSHALL be parameterizable (US12308414B2 Lc versus Ld, including 100 h / 300 h\nasymmetric example). Shared-electrolyte baths of multiple cells SHALL be allowed\n(US12308414B2 FIG. 9A vessel 970).\n","RATIONALE":"EP4602674A1 dual-electrode switch; US12308414B2 dual-cathode and duration scaling.\n","COMMENT":"Cell rhs solves algebraic current/voltage consistency. Outputs include V, I, P, SOC.\n","_LINK":"IA-CEL-001"},"132":{"MID":"f3ccf8523f5841ebb59ff8a58b016e53","UID":"SEC-SSS2-BOP","TITLE":"Thermal, air, and electrolyte hydraulic plant","_LINK":"SEC-SSS2-BOP"},"133":{"MID":"2ba93cc338f34035b9dff645b434687d","UID":"IA-THM-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Thermal ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.thermal SHALL provide class ThermalNetwork.\n\nStates SHALL include thermal energy or temperature at relevant physical nodes.\n\nThe model SHALL calculate conduction, convection, radiation, reaction heat, and cooling.\n\nVessel cavity flow (air or liquid), thermal-mass water fill, and lid-level thermal\nmanagement from US12308414B2 FIGS. 1E-1F and 9A SHALL map onto ThermalNetwork\nnodes. Wide/low vessels SHALL be usable to increase surface area for heat\nrejection.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 double-wall cavity 161 and multi-function lid thermal management.\n","COMMENT":"Adiabatic isolated node with positive heat source increases temperature.\n","_LINK":"IA-THM-001"},"134":{"MID":"eccd9e179efe4bdd9198bbabb3ca25f8","UID":"IA-AIR-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Air handling ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.air_system SHALL provide class AirSystem.\n\nThe component SHALL include gas inventories, manifold pressure dynamics, oxygen\nconcentration, humidity, and airflow coupling.\n\nAir delivery SHALL include natural air-breathing, forced supply, sparging/bubbling\ninto submerged ORR, cascading stacked-core flow, snorkel/test-tube mixed-phase\nchannels, and ramp arrays with holes (US12308414B2 Claims 9, 14-15; FIGS. 37-51,\n71A-71B). Outlet pressure regulation SHALL be able to hold unsealed air-chamber\npressure.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 air-supply and stacked-core architectures. Equal pressure drop across\ndepth-stacked ORR sections is Claim 1.\n","COMMENT":"Manifold pressure rises when inlet mass flow exceeds outlet plus consumption.\n","_LINK":"IA-AIR-001"},"135":{"MID":"c49d257470b04afa806295b6ed8191b4","UID":"IA-AIR-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Fan ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.fan SHALL provide class Fan.\n\nThe model SHALL include rotor speed dynamics and fan power consumption.\n\nFan/blower air SHALL be able to feed submerged ORR stacks and lid air-distribution\nplenums of US12308414B2 FIG. 9A.\n","COMMENT":"Rotor speed is a dynamic state. Electrical power is an algebraic output from speed and torque.\n","_LINK":"IA-AIR-002"},"136":{"MID":"cd85feb54dfc47749f16bc0fdc44e972","UID":"IA-FLD-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte circulation ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.electrolyte_flow SHALL provide class ElectrolyteFlow.\n\nThe component SHALL dynamically track flow, species transfer, pressure or hydraulic\nstorage where physically justified, and temperature.\n","COMMENT":"Species transport delay appears when using the detailed hydraulic mode.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLD-001"},"137":{"MID":"11735e0b13c342d8b8e769131569a782","UID":"IA-FLD-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Pump ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.pump SHALL provide class Pump.\n\nThe component SHALL model motor and hydraulic response, electrical input, and mechanical losses.\n","COMMENT":"Shaft speed is dynamic. Hydraulic power is less than electrical input by documented efficiency.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLD-002"},"138":{"MID":"f886120908274ab2be0890b535fd59d4","UID":"IA-FLD-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reservoir ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.reservoir SHALL provide class Reservoir.\n\nThe component SHALL model liquid inventory, chemical species inventories, temperature,\nand boundary flows.\n","COMMENT":"Mass balance: dM/dt equals inlet minus outlet minus leak.\nThe vessel cavity MAY hold drained electrolyte during maintenance\n(US12308414B2 FIGS. 1E-1F) without requiring off-site storage volume.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLD-003"},"139":{"MID":"3f41093722c34674b68f06e86af48ee2","UID":"IA-FLD-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Valve ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.valve SHALL provide class Valve.\n\nThe component SHALL model actuator response, valve position, and flow-dependent\nhydraulic characteristics.\n","COMMENT":"Position is a dynamic actuator state. Flow is algebraic in position and pressure drop.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLD-004"},"140":{"MID":"953ac04039a342bfbdf5b125ba2cd09c","UID":"IA-FLD-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Piping ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.piping SHALL provide class Pipe.\n\nThe component SHALL model transient fluid transport and temperature or species\nresidence-time dynamics when using the detailed hydraulic mode.\n","COMMENT":"Detailed mode has at least one transport delay state. Lumped mode is flagged reduced_order.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLD-005"},"141":{"MID":"db231d22aa594059a99eaada2e48624a","UID":"IA-HEX-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Heat exchanger ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.heat_exchanger SHALL provide class HeatExchanger.\n\nThe component SHALL represent thermal storage and dynamic heat transfer between fluid circuits.\n","COMMENT":"Two fluid temperatures approach each other under heat transfer with no external loss.\nCavity coolant and lid thermal loops of US12308414B2 SHALL be representable as\nHeatExchanger circuits.\n","_LINK":"IA-HEX-001"},"142":{"MID":"31d73c174b73417db74a7c4b9a68317c","UID":"SEC-SSS2-PLANT","TITLE":"Stack, module, enclosure, and electrical conversion","_LINK":"SEC-SSS2-PLANT"},"143":{"MID":"253387e3a8d745e9a1c3db7215a1dd60","UID":"IA-STK-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stack ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.stack SHALL provide class BatteryStack.\n\nThe component SHALL aggregate cell states while preserving cell-level variations and\nelectrical current conservation.\n\nStacked vessels with conductive contact points SHALL be able to place cells in\nseries (US12308414B2 Claim 3). Arrays of coaxial ORR-core / hexagonal-anode cells\nin a shared electrolyte pool (FIGS. 27-34) and multi-cell housings with dividing\nwalls (FIG. 13) SHALL be supported. Double-sided and air-channel-paired cells\nfrom EP4602674A1 Claims 18-19 SHALL be stackable.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claims 3 and 5, FIGS. 13 and 27-34; EP4602674A1 Claims 18-19.\n","COMMENT":"Series stack current is identical through cells. Terminal voltage is the sum of cell voltages.\n","_LINK":"IA-STK-001"},"144":{"MID":"f8da0a991bbb49a4a0d435c7528b77c1","UID":"IA-MOD-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Module ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.module SHALL provide class BatteryModule.\n\nThe model SHALL include coupled stacks, shared fluid equipment, air equipment, and thermal states.\n\nA module MAY be a stack of 40 housings as illustrated in US12308414B2 FIG. 13,\nsharing lid air distribution, venting, and DC connections (FIG. 9A).\n","COMMENT":"Module assembles at least one stack plus shared thermal or fluid nodes.\n","_LINK":"IA-MOD-001"},"145":{"MID":"12071b6ff1cc442bb257ff16275d7be6","UID":"IA-ENC-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Enclosure ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.enclosure SHALL provide class BatteryEnclosure.\n\nThe model SHALL include enclosure temperature, ventilation, auxiliary electrical loads,\nand enclosure-level gas inventories.\n\nThe enclosure SHALL implement stackable-vessel, double-wall secondary containment,\noptional bag-in-box, trough/dished mechanical shapes, and multi-function lid\n(air distribution, process-gas vent, thermal management, current collection,\ncontrols/sensing, DC connections) from US12308414B2 Claims 3 and 5 and\nFIGS. 1E-1F, 9A, 12A-12F. Alkaline electrolyte SHALL have primary plus secondary\ncontainment.\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 Claim 3 vessel, Claim 5 can-negative housing, hazardous-electrolyte\ncontainment, and lid 902 functions.\n","COMMENT":"Ventilation flow reduces enclosure H2 inventory. Auxiliary load is an algebraic electrical output.\n","_LINK":"IA-ENC-001"},"146":{"MID":"800e8e62f4bf41e39084f24ed3af9c92","UID":"IA-DC-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"DC bus ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.dc_bus SHALL provide class DCBus.\n\nThe component SHALL model DC link capacitor voltage and stored energy.\n","COMMENT":"C * V * dV/dt equals net electrical power into the capacitor.\n","_LINK":"IA-DC-001"},"147":{"MID":"ff5eec6a43104dd09e73c3c2cbcb0b27","UID":"IA-CNV-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"DC/DC converter ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.dc_converter SHALL provide class DCDCConverter.\n\nThe model SHALL include averaged inductor current, capacitor voltage, and converter losses.\n","COMMENT":"Averaged inductor current is a dynamic state. Losses increase output current demand.\n","_LINK":"IA-CNV-001"},"148":{"MID":"d838ba7a6f90434fb4a9b76a02a05689","UID":"IA-INV-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Inverter ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.inverter SHALL provide class GridInverter.\n\nThe model SHALL include averaged electrical control dynamics, DC link interaction,\nand active/reactive power response.\n","COMMENT":"P and Q track references through explicit controller states, not prescribed algebraic jumps only.\n","_LINK":"IA-INV-001"},"149":{"MID":"bcc1e40fe74e457e8ddd0cd133329263","UID":"IA-TRF-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Transformer ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.transformer SHALL provide class Transformer.\n\nThe component SHALL include electromagnetic dynamic states where detailed transient\nmodeling is enabled, along with winding and core thermal dynamics.\n","COMMENT":"Detailed mode includes flux or current states. Thermal states exist in both modes.\nPrescribed grid voltage is an input, not a transformer state.\n","_LINK":"IA-TRF-001"},"150":{"MID":"063175af98e54a5c9803c6aee67eed10","UID":"IA-GRD-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid interface ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.grid SHALL provide class GridInterface.\n\nThe grid SHALL support dynamic voltage and frequency disturbances, network interface\nequations, and configurable external-grid dynamic models.\n\nA prescribed grid voltage or frequency SHALL be treated as an external input, not an\ninvented dynamic state.\n\nGrid-connected LODES dispatch of surplus solar over seasonal windows\n(US12308414B2 Definitions; FIGS. 94-102 bulk energy storage systems) SHALL be\nrepresentable as GridInterface scenarios.\n","COMMENT":"When voltage/frequency are prescribed, they appear in inputs. Optional swing-equation\nmode is explicitly identified.\n","_LINK":"IA-GRD-001"},"151":{"MID":"910a1378af1a4da3b4baa00782688634","UID":"IA-DEG-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Degradation ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.degradation SHALL provide class Degradation.\n\nThe model SHALL represent irreversible material changes, catalyst decay, resistance\ngrowth, and active capacity loss.\n\nORR damage from high OER potential, PTFE hydrophobicity loss, carbonate pore\nclogging, salt crusting on air electrodes, and iron passivation SHALL be\nrepresentable aging modes (US12308414B2 dual-cathode isolation, CO2, and crusting\ndiscussion; EP4602674A1 passivation via Fe(OH)2 films). Seasonal anode replacement\nSHALL be an allowed discrete maintenance event (US12308414B2 replaceable electrodes).\n","COMMENT":"Degradation states are nondecreasing under positive aging rates. Capacity loss is irreversible.\n","_LINK":"IA-DEG-001"},"152":{"MID":"e4802c489f564ab59f947be1a409c617","UID":"IA-FLT-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Sensor ODE","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.components.sensors SHALL provide class DynamicSensor.\n\nThe model SHALL include finite response time, sensor drift, bias, and noise.\n\nLid-mounted reactor controls and sensing of US12308414B2 FIG. 9A SHALL map onto\nDynamicSensor instances for voltage, current, temperature, electrolyte level, gas\nflow, and hydrogen.\n","COMMENT":"First-order sensor lag is a dynamic state. Noise uses a documented random seed.\n","_LINK":"IA-FLT-001"},"153":{"MID":"eb19317c554a4e1493d2ca817006fc76","UID":"IA-CTL-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Supervisory control dynamics","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.controls.supervisory SHALL provide class SupervisoryController.\n\nThe controller SHALL include explicit continuous controller states where applicable,\nas well as discrete operating modes.\n\nIt SHALL be implemented as a hybrid system with ODE dynamics and discrete transitions.\n\nDiscrete modes SHALL include charge (iron-OER-source), discharge (iron-ORR-load),\nrest, protection, and optional airflow-flex isolation of ORR from OER\n(EP4602674A1 switch 116; US12308414B2 switch 550 and Claim 16).\n","COMMENT":"Mode transitions are event-driven. PI or equivalent continuous states exist for current/power regulation.\n","_LINK":"IA-CTL-001"},"154":{"MID":"e7b1d6bc25b2480a80b1ff41718c731b","UID":"IA-EST-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"State estimation","STATEMENT":"Module ironair.controls.estimation SHALL provide class BatteryStateEstimator.\n\nThe model SHALL estimate SOC, SOH, active iron inventory, and available charge/discharge power.\n\nIt SHALL not access the simulated plant's true hidden states during observer execution.\n","COMMENT":"Estimator rhs and update methods accept only measurements and previous estimates,\nnever plant.y hidden components.\n","_LINK":"IA-EST-001"},"155":{"MID":"2e47d320d9004a34b17c2d69a33215d3","UID":"SEC-SSS3-COVER","TITLE":"Scenario suite coverage","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-COVER"},"156":{"MID":"a1bbbbd82abf4589ae91b6acf20d4162","STATEMENT":"Operating scenarios exercise the patented iron-air plant. Charge, discharge, and\nLODES durations follow US12308414B2 (8 h to seasonal, including about 100 h and\n300 h). Chemistry faults follow EP4602674A1 HER competition, magnetite conversion,\nand thick-electrode transport. Air and mechanical faults follow US12308414B2\nflooding, dry-out, sparging, fan/pump, and containment disclosures. Software\nscenario results are not hardware validation of either patent.\n","_LINK":"a1bbbbd82abf4589ae91b6acf20d4162"},"157":{"MID":"cbca929605f54bf6906f3b540314df7c","UID":"IA-NBK-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required charge scenarios: constant-current, constant-power, constant-voltage,\ncharge tapering, partial charge, charge termination, overcharge protection,\nlow-temperature charging, high-temperature charging, charging with hydrogen\nevolution, and charging with limited electrolyte circulation.\n\nCharge current greater than 6 mA/g to at least twice rated capacity SHALL be an\nallowed formation/full-charge protocol (EP4602674A1 [0261]). Dual-electrode charge\nSHALL use the OER path (EP4602674A1 [0049]).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 rechargeable metal-air operation; EP4602674A1 charge through OER and\nHER competition on thick iron electrodes.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-CHG-* ID, Python scenario function, notebook,\nand seaborn plots.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-007"},"158":{"MID":"6bda0fec75ba46c8b98577fa7681b1b1","UID":"IA-NBK-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Discharge scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required discharge scenarios: constant-current, constant-power, variable-load,\npartial discharge, deep discharge, oxygen-limited, thermal-limited, high-rate,\nlow-rate, and 100-hour discharge.\n\nDischarge SHALL use the ORR path (EP4602674A1 [0049]). 100 h and 300 h discharges\nSHALL be available, corresponding to about 3-5 cm and 4-6 cm anode thickness in\nUS12308414B2. Oxygen-limited discharge SHALL include restricted pO2 within\n0.01-100 atm (US12308414B2).\n","RATIONALE":"US12308414B2 duration versus anode thickness; EP4602674A1 dual-electrode discharge\nto Fe(OH)2 / Fe3O4.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-DCH-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-008"},"159":{"MID":"f2004c21cfb84e1dbd0f166fbefa7163","UID":"IA-NBK-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Transition scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required transition scenarios: charge to discharge, discharge to charge, rapid power\nreversal, rest period, voltage relaxation, repeated cycling, startup, shutdown, and\nrestart after protection trip.\n\nCharge/discharge transitions SHALL operate the EP4602674A1 [0049] / US12308414B2\nFIGS. 5A-5B electrode switch, including electrical isolation of ORR during OER\nto protect the air electrode.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-TRN-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-009"},"160":{"MID":"c8ee29880f0d493ba5d68156aeab179d","UID":"IA-NBK-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Chemistry scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required chemistry scenarios: iron passivation, magnetite formation, hydrogen evolution,\noxygen starvation, carbonate accumulation, water depletion, electrolyte dilution,\nelectrolyte concentration increase, electrode aging, and catalyst degradation.\n\nChemistry scenarios SHALL include planar versus channeled versus interdigitated\ncoulombic-efficiency comparison at 6 M KOH and 303 K (EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-27)\nand carbonate clogging of air-electrode pores (US12308414B2).\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-CHM-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-010"},"161":{"MID":"ce3523685f53480597e8b0be09d53dc6","UID":"IA-NBK-011","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Environmental scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required environmental scenarios: cold start, hot start, ambient temperature cycling,\nhigh relative humidity, low relative humidity, high altitude, reduced atmospheric\noxygen, and thermal control failure.\n\nEnvironmental cases SHALL include US12308414B2 25 C / 1 atm reference, EP4602674A1\n303 K KOH reference, high-humidity flooding of GDE, low-humidity dry-out, and\nreduced pO2.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-ENV-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-011"},"162":{"MID":"a84f97b85cc644a4aa0bb4f691ddd086","UID":"IA-NBK-012","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Mechanical fault scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required mechanical fault scenarios: fan failure, pump failure, filter blockage,\nvalve failure, electrolyte leak, reservoir depletion, reduced circulation, heat\nexchanger failure, and ventilation failure.\n\nMechanical faults SHALL include loss of sparge/air supply to submerged ORR, ORR\nflooding versus dry-out, lid-vent blockage, and primary-vessel leak into secondary\ncontainment (US12308414B2 vessel cavity 161).\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-MCH-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-012"},"163":{"MID":"07fe5c29401e42ec81d668ca2fe3240e","UID":"IA-NBK-013","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrical scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required electrical scenarios: cell short, cell open circuit, cell imbalance, stack\nmismatch, converter overload, DC bus undervoltage, DC bus overvoltage, inverter\ncurrent limit, and sensor failure.\n\nElectrical scenarios SHALL include can-negative housing contact faults, ORR/OER\ntoggle-switch stuck states, and stacked-vessel series-contact opens\n(US12308414B2 Claims 3, 5, 16).\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-ELC-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-013"},"164":{"MID":"3cae934595f24fe5ba9e6330273b9a87","UID":"IA-NBK-014","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required grid scenarios: voltage sag, voltage swell, underfrequency, overfrequency,\nfrequency-watt response, volt-var response, grid disconnection, reconnection,\nride-through, and active/reactive power limits.\n\nGrid scenarios SHALL cover LODES services from milliseconds to seasonal windows\nas framed in US12308414B2 Background and FIGS. 94-102.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-GRD-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-014"},"165":{"MID":"8ffff82016fa46aeaafd7e2924732276","UID":"IA-NBK-015","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Long-term scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required long-term scenarios: 100-hour discharge, multiple-day energy dispatch,\ncharge/discharge cycling, seasonal storage, calendar aging, cycle aging, capacity\nfade, efficiency degradation, and annual operating profile.\n\nLong-term scenarios SHALL include 100 h and 300 h discharges, summer-to-winter\nseasonal storage of surplus solar, and calendar aging of replaceable electrodes\n(US12308414B2 Definitions and replaceable-electrode discussion).\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-LT-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-015"},"166":{"MID":"7d5f5f7130c0425491a8fbe4ae6326e2","UID":"IA-NBK-016","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Characterization scenarios","STATEMENT":"Required characterization scenarios: current-voltage polarization curves,\ncharge/discharge voltage versus SOC, efficiency versus power, efficiency versus\ntemperature, SOC versus time, oxygen demand versus power, hydrogen generation versus\ncharge current, KOH concentration sweep, thermal response, parameter sensitivity,\nand uncertainty analysis.\n\nCharacterization SHALL include planar versus interdigitated discharge voltage\nversus capacity (EP4602674A1 FIG. 27), coulombic efficiency versus charge time\n(FIGS. 19, 21, 23, 25), and area-specific resistance versus time (FIGS. 20, 22,\n24, 26) at documented 6 M KOH and 303 K.\n","COMMENT":"Each listed scenario has a unique IA-SCN-CHR-* ID, Python scenario function, and notebook.\n","_LINK":"IA-NBK-016"},"167":{"MID":"28bc57ab81b742d8b3221bd24965cdb5","UID":"SEC-SSS3-CHG","TITLE":"Charge scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-CHG"},"168":{"MID":"d7e8ecebebe24edfac80604bff1b1d43","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-CC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constant-current charging","STATEMENT":"Apply a constant charging current and record voltage, SOC, HER, and temperature.\n","COMMENT":"SOC increases. Terminal voltage rises. Current remains at the commanded charge value\nuntil a termination limit.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-CC"},"169":{"MID":"3b3b38d22eba4e20a930c3a281edf397","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-CP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constant-power charging","STATEMENT":"Apply a constant charging power command.\n","COMMENT":"Electrical power tracks the charge setpoint within solver tolerance until limits.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-CP"},"170":{"MID":"5e44938f4c774721a207a98f3bef2a4a","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-CV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constant-voltage charging","STATEMENT":"Hold a constant charge voltage after a current-limited approach.\n","COMMENT":"Charge current tapers as SOC increases under CV hold.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-CV"},"171":{"MID":"5ee200b34fbd4f098ba5cfb6ad0551de","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-TAPER","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge tapering","STATEMENT":"Execute CC-CV tapering to a cutoff current.\n","COMMENT":"Simulation stops or flags complete when abs(I) falls below cutoff while voltage is held.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-TAPER"},"172":{"MID":"7f2d745e18924b9485b96efa847a6c17","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-PARTIAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Partial charge","STATEMENT":"Charge from an intermediate SOC to a higher intermediate SOC.\n","COMMENT":"Final SOC is between the start SOC and 1.0 and matches the scheduled stop.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-PARTIAL"},"173":{"MID":"dddb5f842dab414fac84de1135b2da14","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-TERM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge termination","STATEMENT":"Terminate charge on voltage, current, SOC, or hydrogen-rate limits.\n","COMMENT":"Supervisory controller enters a rest or protection mode when a documented limit is crossed.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-TERM"},"174":{"MID":"602d1c6d0db345308523bcce3aaa915b","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-OVER","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Overcharge protection","STATEMENT":"Attempt overcharge and verify protection action.\n","COMMENT":"Current is removed or limited after the overcharge threshold. HER may rise before trip.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-OVER"},"175":{"MID":"731cd0e074d34381b15a77da6b54a347","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-LOWT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Low-temperature charging","STATEMENT":"Charge at a cold ambient temperature within the property-model domain.\n","COMMENT":"Polarization is larger than the 298 K reference case. Solver remains stable.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-LOWT"},"176":{"MID":"8f26f6aee5a34c459beab7e24c8a3cd8","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-HIGHT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"High-temperature charging","STATEMENT":"Charge at an elevated ambient temperature within the property-model domain.\n","COMMENT":"HER rate is higher than the 298 K reference at the same current.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-HIGHT"},"177":{"MID":"cc9e2c2a61d94c969f82d9072b706cf4","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-HER","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charging with hydrogen evolution","STATEMENT":"Charge under conditions that produce measurable HER.\n","COMMENT":"H2 inventory increases. Faradaic efficiency of iron conversion is less than 1.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-HER"},"178":{"MID":"ee658f41d8c24e60939f34f8e434a3f0","UID":"IA-SCN-CHG-LOWFLOW","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charging with limited electrolyte circulation","STATEMENT":"Charge with reduced or zero electrolyte pump flow.\n","COMMENT":"Local concentration gradients or voltage penalty exceed the circulating reference case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHG-LOWFLOW"},"179":{"MID":"55774142318b4f88a610c387c246367e","UID":"SEC-SSS3-DCH","TITLE":"Discharge scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-DCH"},"180":{"MID":"f44a01ef1ca24ba2b54af2822ba9ef54","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-CC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constant-current discharge","STATEMENT":"Apply a constant discharge current.\n","COMMENT":"SOC decreases. Voltage falls. Iron inventory is oxidized.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-CC"},"181":{"MID":"261496e9031747149e796101eb7990cb","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-CP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constant-power discharge","STATEMENT":"Apply a constant discharge power command.\n","COMMENT":"Power tracks the discharge setpoint until a voltage or SOC limit.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-CP"},"182":{"MID":"e9250185b56c411bbfec691d1b8dc1f7","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-VAR","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Variable-load discharge","STATEMENT":"Apply a time-varying discharge load profile.\n","COMMENT":"Current follows the scheduled profile within solver tolerance.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-VAR"},"183":{"MID":"0eb5028956904abcb78bdf046efcd1eb","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-PARTIAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Partial discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge from a high SOC to an intermediate SOC.\n","COMMENT":"Final SOC matches the scheduled stop within tolerance.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-PARTIAL"},"184":{"MID":"0e06716dc845461bb91a8303370c5d53","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-DEEP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Deep discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge toward a low-SOC cutoff.\n","COMMENT":"Voltage declines and a low-SOC or low-voltage stop is reached without negative inventories.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-DEEP"},"185":{"MID":"0429476585ba4d54a4352670b5d7852d","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-O2LIM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen-limited discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge with restricted oxygen supply.\n","COMMENT":"Available discharge current or voltage is lower than the air-rich reference.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-O2LIM"},"186":{"MID":"bb74b5058d834729becc822671ea6326","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-THMLIM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Thermal-limited discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge until a thermal protection limit.\n","COMMENT":"Controller reduces current or trips when temperature exceeds the documented limit.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-THMLIM"},"187":{"MID":"2bfa926c9c4f4371b3539e216e779b3b","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-HIGHRATE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"High-rate discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge at a high C-rate relative to the design current.\n","COMMENT":"Overpotential and heat generation exceed the low-rate case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-HIGHRATE"},"188":{"MID":"0ff222f7fc934742be294ee088c8b82f","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-LOWRATE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Low-rate discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge at a low C-rate.\n","COMMENT":"Voltage remains closer to Nernst than the high-rate case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-LOWRATE"},"189":{"MID":"3cc89f4fe44e4301b48e946eceb1133c","UID":"IA-SCN-DCH-100H","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"100-hour discharge","STATEMENT":"Discharge energy over a 100-hour window.\n","COMMENT":"Simulation spans 3.6e5 s and SOC declines monotonically under a positive discharge load.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-DCH-100H"},"190":{"MID":"dba4be8760ad49ea933a99f3d9d74d67","UID":"SEC-SSS3-TRN","TITLE":"Transition scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-TRN"},"191":{"MID":"6d202e0220904fb9916d0c6abba5f5e7","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-CHG2DCH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge to discharge","STATEMENT":"Transition from charge to discharge.\n","COMMENT":"Current sign reverses. No nonphysical voltage spike is left unreported.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-CHG2DCH"},"192":{"MID":"e2775870c86e4869a4e8e040785859f4","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-DCH2CHG","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Discharge to charge","STATEMENT":"Transition from discharge to charge.\n","COMMENT":"Current sign reverses. SOC slope changes sign.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-DCH2CHG"},"193":{"MID":"3ac2db0720324fd3b1156a5fb258f8a1","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-REVERSAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Rapid power reversal","STATEMENT":"Reverse power on a short time scale relative to thermal states.\n","COMMENT":"Electrical states respond faster than thermal states. Solver remains stable.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-REVERSAL"},"194":{"MID":"c2da560b30d5483c87f75f48be88e3e6","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-REST","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Rest period","STATEMENT":"Hold zero applied current after operation.\n","COMMENT":"Terminal current is ~0. Voltage relaxes toward equilibrium.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-REST"},"195":{"MID":"bc827a36e9284740a099d63a9bb4ed20","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-RELAX","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Voltage relaxation","STATEMENT":"Record open-circuit voltage relaxation after current interruption.\n","COMMENT":"The absolute value of dV/dt decreases with time during rest.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-RELAX"},"196":{"MID":"831889264eba4f8d837d2df7687a2c6b","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-CYCLE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Repeated cycling","STATEMENT":"Apply repeated charge/discharge cycles.\n","COMMENT":"SOC oscillates. Optional degradation states are nondecreasing.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-CYCLE"},"197":{"MID":"2a2e3207a7d746e9b90f721c3a0e7003","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-STARTUP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Startup","STATEMENT":"Start auxiliaries and electrical conversion from a rest state.\n","COMMENT":"Fan/pump speeds rise from near zero. DC bus voltage is established.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-STARTUP"},"198":{"MID":"50ce923815044f3cb4d8bfdc03fb23d9","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-SHUTDOWN","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Shutdown","STATEMENT":"Command a controlled shutdown.\n","COMMENT":"Power command goes to zero and auxiliaries decelerate.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-SHUTDOWN"},"199":{"MID":"e001c1f66e58485f807e8a80c77313b4","UID":"IA-SCN-TRN-RESTART","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Restart after protection trip","STATEMENT":"Clear a protection trip and resume operation.\n","COMMENT":"Mode returns from protection to a documented operating mode without hidden-state cheating.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-TRN-RESTART"},"200":{"MID":"ac751564a8ec47d2bd481de50c1a5ea0","UID":"SEC-SSS3-CHM","TITLE":"Chemistry scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-CHM"},"201":{"MID":"0369709b8a78499caf9a61a42bc3776a","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-PASS","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Iron passivation","STATEMENT":"Exercise passivation-thickness growth.\n","COMMENT":"Active area or exchange current declines as passivation thickness grows.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-PASS"},"202":{"MID":"5a41f62eb79b4eea9fd052da66919ca8","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-MAG","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Magnetite formation","STATEMENT":"Convert Fe(OH)2 toward Fe3O4.\n","COMMENT":"Fe3O4 inventory increases and Fe(OH)2 decreases with conserved iron atoms.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-MAG"},"203":{"MID":"e5bb8c1d64204b8e87c7b4ffd4e5119f","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-HER","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hydrogen evolution chemistry scenario","STATEMENT":"Isolate HER kinetics versus potential.\n","COMMENT":"H2 production increases as iron potential becomes more negative.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-HER"},"204":{"MID":"08289068f685424483328bffdff4cb2b","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-O2STARV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen starvation","STATEMENT":"Remove oxygen supply during discharge.\n","COMMENT":"ORR current collapses as O2 inventory approaches the documented floor.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-O2STARV"},"205":{"MID":"c3cb41e0dedb4b3eabff9d2147ded88c","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-CARB","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Carbonate accumulation","STATEMENT":"Expose electrolyte to CO2-bearing air.\n","COMMENT":"CO3 inventory increases and OH- inventory decreases.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-CARB"},"206":{"MID":"708acc346c5b4c34bb92e66cad5b8cef","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-H2ODEP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Water depletion","STATEMENT":"Operate with net water consumption or evaporation.\n","COMMENT":"Water inventory declines and is flagged before becoming negative.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-H2ODEP"},"207":{"MID":"355ea3014f3a447092151a6e6dcf1d65","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-DILUTE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte dilution","STATEMENT":"Add water or reduce KOH inventory.\n","COMMENT":"KOH concentration and conductivity decrease.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-DILUTE"},"208":{"MID":"8fd0b42e95034394a18bd3f3943397c2","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-CONC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte concentration increase","STATEMENT":"Remove water or add KOH.\n","COMMENT":"KOH concentration increases within the property-model domain.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-CONC"},"209":{"MID":"f0fa387aa5d042b0a7bec3d0b0220621","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-AGE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrode aging","STATEMENT":"Apply aging rates to iron active material.\n","COMMENT":"Available capacity declines irreversibly.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-AGE"},"210":{"MID":"481789e1273145dd9d31220a9cabf0f4","UID":"IA-SCN-CHM-CATDEG","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Catalyst degradation","STATEMENT":"Decay ORR/OER catalyst activity.\n","COMMENT":"Exchange current densities decrease over time under the aging model.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHM-CATDEG"},"211":{"MID":"4e478f1f0b9f4f11b53f2ed9466814e7","UID":"SEC-SSS3-ENV","TITLE":"Environmental scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-ENV"},"212":{"MID":"8214bea784394ceb8c28f1957a9064d8","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-COLD","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Cold start","STATEMENT":"Start from a cold soaked initial temperature.\n","COMMENT":"Temperatures rise toward a warmer operating band under load or heaters if modeled.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-COLD"},"213":{"MID":"1d337cd1aca94b26a76fb2c7009985e9","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-HOT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hot start","STATEMENT":"Start from an elevated soaked temperature.\n","COMMENT":"Initial temperatures are above T_REF_K and remain within validity limits or are flagged.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-HOT"},"214":{"MID":"bde19dfa3f464ec39d64ecd24dd21108","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-TAMP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Ambient temperature cycling","STATEMENT":"Apply a cyclic ambient temperature boundary.\n","COMMENT":"Enclosure or cell temperature lags the ambient cycle.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-TAMP"},"215":{"MID":"a2c8f2e33d024009ab77297fb568099c","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-HIGHRH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"High relative humidity","STATEMENT":"Supply high-humidity air.\n","COMMENT":"Gas humidity state increases relative to a dry-air case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-HIGHRH"},"216":{"MID":"3d28690f40ed4cbab0d2a81fe7099e6b","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-LOWRH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Low relative humidity","STATEMENT":"Supply dry air.\n","COMMENT":"Electrolyte or GDL water is more depleted than the high-RH case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-LOWRH"},"217":{"MID":"56b1ad2f8533463e8e24fc823fc6d7f8","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-ALT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"High altitude","STATEMENT":"Reduce ambient pressure.\n","COMMENT":"Inlet oxygen partial pressure is lower than the sea-level reference.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-ALT"},"218":{"MID":"f50b03f64dde43548061705888e8c98a","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-LOWO2","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reduced atmospheric oxygen","STATEMENT":"Reduce inlet O2 mole fraction.\n","COMMENT":"Discharge capability is reduced versus 0.21 mole fraction air.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-LOWO2"},"219":{"MID":"516e0abd607b4f118b846f65975a3fb4","UID":"IA-SCN-ENV-THMFAIL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Thermal control failure","STATEMENT":"Disable cooling while load continues.\n","COMMENT":"Temperatures rise above the cooled reference. Protection may trip.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ENV-THMFAIL"},"220":{"MID":"7f89fef87df0473dbed078eddf399d56","UID":"SEC-SSS3-MCH","TITLE":"Mechanical fault scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-MCH"},"221":{"MID":"220fcf39fcea4edaada42427fcef049b","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-FAN","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Fan failure","STATEMENT":"Force fan speed toward zero during operation.\n","COMMENT":"Airflow and O2 delivery fall. Discharge voltage penalty increases.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-FAN"},"222":{"MID":"aee5678d61174fcf84b950f393ab2ab7","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-PUMP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Pump failure","STATEMENT":"Force pump speed toward zero.\n","COMMENT":"Electrolyte circulation collapses.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-PUMP"},"223":{"MID":"7e66cffcd0a741d795cf33fd10cb1e09","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-FILTER","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Filter blockage","STATEMENT":"Increase air-path resistance.\n","COMMENT":"Airflow falls for the same fan command.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-FILTER"},"224":{"MID":"429a09b219f44ad89960ad541c6e7008","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-VALVE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Valve failure","STATEMENT":"Hold a valve at a failed position.\n","COMMENT":"Flow does not follow the commanded position.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-VALVE"},"225":{"MID":"65ddeba3ecef46f9a68c67d553ccadc4","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-LEAK","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte leak","STATEMENT":"Impose a leak mass-flow from the electrolyte circuit.\n","COMMENT":"Liquid inventory declines.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-LEAK"},"226":{"MID":"27d58f3d99744c0398ad5af2d11c6157","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-RESDEP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reservoir depletion","STATEMENT":"Operate until reservoir inventory approaches empty.\n","COMMENT":"An invalid or empty-reservoir condition is reported before negative mass.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-RESDEP"},"227":{"MID":"28cc3d4b2d2d48668b64a4100d8dae7d","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-CIRC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Reduced circulation","STATEMENT":"Command a reduced pump flow.\n","COMMENT":"Circulation flow is lower than the design case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-CIRC"},"228":{"MID":"62e124b489c44e29a3fe068070f9029e","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-HEX","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Heat exchanger failure","STATEMENT":"Reduce heat-exchanger UA toward zero.\n","COMMENT":"Cooling heat transfer falls versus the healthy HEX case.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-HEX"},"229":{"MID":"cbac68288a9741b4aec98adaa8479d8c","UID":"IA-SCN-MCH-VENT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Ventilation failure","STATEMENT":"Disable enclosure ventilation.\n","COMMENT":"Enclosure gas inventories of H2 or heat rise relative to ventilated operation.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-MCH-VENT"},"230":{"MID":"bea050a7168c4401a091ba63eb496afb","UID":"SEC-SSS3-ELC","TITLE":"Electrical scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-ELC"},"231":{"MID":"ec37335d21084e13b0347db5fe16e12e","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-SHORT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Cell short","STATEMENT":"Impose a low-resistance cell short.\n","COMMENT":"Faulted cell voltage collapses and local heating increases.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-SHORT"},"232":{"MID":"7ee0209b5da641afa96a75b0b6ac0d41","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-OC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Cell open circuit","STATEMENT":"Open a cell in a series string.\n","COMMENT":"String current falls to ~0 or the open-cell path is flagged.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-OC"},"233":{"MID":"f49428b17df049caab33641619204a5d","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-IMBAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Cell imbalance","STATEMENT":"Initialize cells at different SOC.\n","COMMENT":"Cell voltages differ at the same string current.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-IMBAL"},"234":{"MID":"d9fd258d8aca40e5a6286ad518c73a93","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-MISMATCH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stack mismatch","STATEMENT":"Operate paralleled or series stacks with mismatched capacity or resistance.\n","COMMENT":"Current sharing is unequal.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-MISMATCH"},"235":{"MID":"84be7cfc3ae146f38ec5dde19ed006b0","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-CNVOVL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Converter overload","STATEMENT":"Command converter current above rating.\n","COMMENT":"Current is limited to the rated value or a protection trip occurs.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-CNVOVL"},"236":{"MID":"9acf308118e549aab335a1bec9fc2160","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-UV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"DC bus undervoltage","STATEMENT":"Drive DC-link voltage below the undervoltage threshold.\n","COMMENT":"Protection or limiter responds at the documented UV setpoint.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-UV"},"237":{"MID":"b5442985c0a244afb8f08d910fa9357b","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-OV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"DC bus overvoltage","STATEMENT":"Drive DC-link voltage above the overvoltage threshold.\n","COMMENT":"Protection or limiter responds at the documented OV setpoint.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-OV"},"238":{"MID":"4327b2e1e75745bd806367d9f8a93a2c","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-ILIM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Inverter current limit","STATEMENT":"Command inverter current above the limit.\n","COMMENT":"Delivered current does not exceed the limit except for documented transient overshoot.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-ILIM"},"239":{"MID":"02ae48fd495349119ff021c846c1aa6f","UID":"IA-SCN-ELC-SENSOR","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Sensor failure","STATEMENT":"Inject sensor bias, stuck, or noise faults.\n","COMMENT":"Estimator and controller use the failed measurement, not the hidden true plant state.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-ELC-SENSOR"},"240":{"MID":"29bfaa592fab4ce8b3dfe5a348a74544","UID":"SEC-SSS3-GRD","TITLE":"Grid scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-GRD"},"241":{"MID":"4872faf1da404f3796996176b194a0d3","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-SAG","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid voltage sag","STATEMENT":"Apply a grid voltage sag as an external input.\n","COMMENT":"Grid voltage is not a fabricated dynamic state when prescribed. Ride-through monitors record the event.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-SAG"},"242":{"MID":"c13d0c3f245b48e2bc143333d3886a96","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-SWELL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid voltage swell","STATEMENT":"Apply a grid voltage swell as an external input.\n","COMMENT":"Prescribed swell appears in inputs. Inverter reactive response follows the configured Q(V) curve if enabled.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-SWELL"},"243":{"MID":"298f6ce0788e48819b23d8dee8ad484c","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-UF","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Underfrequency","STATEMENT":"Apply a grid underfrequency input.\n","COMMENT":"Frequency is an input unless swing-equation mode is explicitly enabled.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-UF"},"244":{"MID":"7ae168830c6145d6b791793f1804fcee","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-OF","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Overfrequency","STATEMENT":"Apply a grid overfrequency input.\n","COMMENT":"Frequency-watt reduction of active power occurs when enabled.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-OF"},"245":{"MID":"edbd0b635340443894e49f2320cf41de","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-FW","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Frequency-watt response","STATEMENT":"Exercise P(f) droop.\n","COMMENT":"Active power decreases for overfrequency according to the configured droop.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-FW"},"246":{"MID":"a80f3aeeac054126adf846b129cd98b8","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-VV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Volt-var response","STATEMENT":"Exercise Q(V) droop.\n","COMMENT":"Reactive power sign follows the configured volt-var curve.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-VV"},"247":{"MID":"d040c8bf1b004a9d89b13da5b829c395","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-DISC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid disconnection","STATEMENT":"Open the grid interconnect.\n","COMMENT":"Grid current goes to ~0. Plant enters island or shutdown per controller mode.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-DISC"},"248":{"MID":"565d68f05db14af6bf92f87718e0aa4b","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-RECON","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Grid reconnection","STATEMENT":"Reconnect after a disconnect.\n","COMMENT":"Power exchange resumes only after documented sync/permission conditions.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-RECON"},"249":{"MID":"6784ff0ff4af4a5ba7e50db19dfca6b1","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-RT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Ride-through","STATEMENT":"Remain connected through a documented disturbance envelope.\n","COMMENT":"Trip does not occur inside the configured ride-through region.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-RT"},"250":{"MID":"082c68e4970c477ea1a1ad6440dce8b4","UID":"IA-SCN-GRD-PQ","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Active/reactive power limits","STATEMENT":"Command P and Q outside the capability curve.\n","COMMENT":"Delivered P/Q remain inside the capability region.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-GRD-PQ"},"251":{"MID":"c4f5e5c326e34e1fb91e3b3d40c11a14","UID":"SEC-SSS3-LT","TITLE":"Long-term scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-LT"},"252":{"MID":"49353ca82dff4a8c9decdac19cb216d7","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-100H","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Long-term 100-hour discharge","STATEMENT":"Run the 100-hour discharge as a long-horizon scenario sharing cell parameters.\n","COMMENT":"Uses the same ParameterSet as short-horizon discharge, with fidelity settings documented.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-100H"},"253":{"MID":"e82d54f7823941a1a46f069028814d8a","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-DISPATCH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Multiple-day energy dispatch","STATEMENT":"Follow a multi-day power schedule.\n","COMMENT":"SOC tracks charge and discharge windows in the schedule.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-DISPATCH"},"254":{"MID":"cdc67631c1744ae9be08e0d8c49c1b39","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-CYCLE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Charge/discharge cycling long-term","STATEMENT":"Cycle for many days.\n","COMMENT":"Cycle count increases. Optional fade is nondecreasing.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-CYCLE"},"255":{"MID":"d3a96e50177640bf952678c03db6d70e","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-SEASON","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Seasonal storage","STATEMENT":"Hold energy over a seasonal horizon using reduced-order options as needed.\n","COMMENT":"Calendar aging can proceed while cycling rate is low. Reduced-order use is flagged.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-SEASON"},"256":{"MID":"5741487e2fa54c5ca847e05425e2238d","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-CAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Calendar aging","STATEMENT":"Age at rest.\n","COMMENT":"Capacity or resistance changes with time at zero cycling current.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-CAL"},"257":{"MID":"78ec845b131448b0a379bf420fee4cf1","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-CYCAGE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Cycle aging","STATEMENT":"Age under cycling.\n","COMMENT":"Fade rate exceeds the rest-only calendar case for the same elapsed time.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-CYCAGE"},"258":{"MID":"505722fa6fe242d4981cdd5d867faa94","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-FADE","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Capacity fade","STATEMENT":"Track available capacity versus time or throughput.\n","COMMENT":"Reported capacity is nonincreasing under the aging model.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-FADE"},"259":{"MID":"dd079b15bcb64ef39afae5a58b4b6925","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-EFF","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Efficiency degradation","STATEMENT":"Track round-trip efficiency versus aging state.\n","COMMENT":"Efficiency declines as resistance grows.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-EFF"},"260":{"MID":"8a4adaacec52428ebed451e883c0ac93","UID":"IA-SCN-LT-ANNUAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Annual operating profile","STATEMENT":"Execute a year-scale operating profile with documented fidelity.\n","COMMENT":"Scenario metadata records duration and fidelity setting. Parameters are shared with cell models.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-LT-ANNUAL"},"261":{"MID":"1842ce2833e54e6bb9a8e893098dea80","UID":"SEC-SSS3-CHR","TITLE":"Characterization scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-CHR"},"262":{"MID":"c615df8ad0c2494d826d72805d2c2880","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-IV","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Current-voltage polarization","STATEMENT":"Sweep current and record voltage.\n","COMMENT":"Discharge branch voltage decreases with increasing current magnitude.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-IV"},"263":{"MID":"01407128b2be444aa3c186815d2f3226","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-VSOC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Voltage versus SOC","STATEMENT":"Record charge and discharge voltage versus SOC.\n","COMMENT":"Charge voltage lies above discharge voltage at the same SOC for nonzero current.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-VSOC"},"264":{"MID":"c70ce36d8d874a8bafe4179d2addbccd","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-EFFP","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Efficiency versus power","STATEMENT":"Compute round-trip or one-way efficiency versus power.\n","COMMENT":"Efficiency is in (0, 1] for finite nonzero power.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-EFFP"},"265":{"MID":"288b5b1b636749f7ab74414aaa0cdef1","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-EFFT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Efficiency versus temperature","STATEMENT":"Sweep temperature and compute efficiency.\n","COMMENT":"Results stay within the property-model temperature domain.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-EFFT"},"266":{"MID":"a3f3d9042105460abb438e0889975fa4","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-SOCT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"SOC versus time","STATEMENT":"Record SOC(t) for a documented load.\n","COMMENT":"SOC is in [0, 1] and consistent with Faraday accounting.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-SOCT"},"267":{"MID":"0393483e5c264b3db0f6ab3d2df6f270","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-O2P","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Oxygen demand versus power","STATEMENT":"Compute O2 molar demand versus discharge power.\n","COMMENT":"O2 demand increases with discharge power under ORR Faraday accounting.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-O2P"},"268":{"MID":"2b007622135142778ee7831ee069c903","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-H2I","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Hydrogen generation versus charge current","STATEMENT":"Compute H2 rate versus charge current.\n","COMMENT":"H2 rate is nonnegative and increases with more severe charge polarization.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-H2I"},"269":{"MID":"0caf991ff93747a6803d1705de80c245","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-KOH","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"KOH concentration sweep","STATEMENT":"Sweep KOH concentration through the property domain.\n","COMMENT":"Conductivity and Nernst potentials vary with concentration. Domain errors are raised outside range.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-KOH"},"270":{"MID":"5253b3040e2b4a96826ef12cf6bdab1f","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-THM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Thermal response characterization","STATEMENT":"Record temperature response to a load step.\n","COMMENT":"Temperature rises under dissipative load in an undercooled configuration.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-THM"},"271":{"MID":"7d11a4513f4f4c91b833934f6d3ff676","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-SENS","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Parameter sensitivity","STATEMENT":"Perturb selected parameters and record output change.\n","COMMENT":"Sensitivity results identify which parameters were perturbed and their source_class.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-SENS"},"272":{"MID":"6b93498765774db2af5c5421f249c67e","UID":"IA-SCN-CHR-UNC","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Uncertainty analysis","STATEMENT":"Propagate documented parameter uncertainties with a fixed random seed.\n","COMMENT":"Results are reproducible given the same seed and parameter uncertainty data.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-CHR-UNC"},"273":{"MID":"30b074badb1e4f1a897320332481737f","UID":"SEC-SSS3-PAT","TITLE":"Patent-architecture scenarios","_LINK":"SEC-SSS3-PAT"},"274":{"MID":"6f8856af4b60498386a04a58e7589e48","STATEMENT":"These scenarios map US12308414B2 Claims 1-18 and EP4602674A1 Claims 1, 20, 38, 55,\n70, 77, and FIGS. 19-27 onto executable ironair scenario functions.\n","_LINK":"6f8856af4b60498386a04a58e7589e48"},"275":{"MID":"2fb5b036ea5a4f94a5a73f0bd423bd8a","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-STACKORR","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Submerged stacked ORR equal pressure drop","STATEMENT":"Operate a vertical stack of discrete submerged ORR sections with depth-dependent\ngeometry and equal pressure drop in the depth direction (US12308414B2 Claim 1).\nRecord air utilization, pO2, and ORR current versus height.\n","COMMENT":"Pressure drop across each stacked section is the same. Bubble cascade reaches\nupper sections. No external electrolyte leak is required for internal flooding.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-STACKORR"},"276":{"MID":"e2982091cc534e0eadab3241f25022f9","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-DRI","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"DRI pellet bed anode","STATEMENT":"Discharge and charge a packed bed of direct reduced iron pellets\n(US12308414B2 Claim 18; FIG. 3) in 6 M KOH.\n","COMMENT":"Iron inventory oxidizes to Fe(OH)2 / Fe3O4 on discharge. Pellet-bed porosity\nabsorbs discharge volume increase (EP4602674A1 [0043]).\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-DRI"},"277":{"MID":"1d1b9fcec3ed466f9750f5cd8e1a26ff","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-INTERDIG","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Interdigitated OER versus planar control","STATEMENT":"Compare planar iron/OER, channeled iron with planar OER, and channeled iron with\ninterdigitated OER at 6 M KOH, 303 K, initial rib porosity 0.6, ChanFrac 0.4,\nw_rib 5 mm, and 4 g Fe/cm2 (EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-27).\n","COMMENT":"Interdigitated configuration shows higher coulombic efficiency and an extended\nupper discharge voltage plateau relative to planar, matching FIG. 27 qualitative\nbehavior. Software comparison is verification against the disclosed trends, not\nreproduction of unpublished experimental tables.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-INTERDIG"},"278":{"MID":"824b7fc1f4fe4e9caf6cd9fd0ba72d90","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-ASYM","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Asymmetric 100 h charge and 300 h discharge","STATEMENT":"Configure independent charge and discharge length scales for about 100 h charge\nand about 300 h discharge with a 5 cm by 5 cm anode cross-section\n(US12308414B2 asymmetric-duration example).\n","COMMENT":"Charge duration and discharge duration are independently tunable. SOC and Faraday\naccounting remain consistent over both windows.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-ASYM"},"279":{"MID":"f3d14cbb8fc34037a088fbaf55d2a79a","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-FLOAT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Floating ORR with submerged OER","STATEMENT":"Float the ORR at the electrolyte free surface while the OER remains submerged\n(US12308414B2 Summary; FIGS. 1B-3). Vary electrolyte height by evaporation and\nconfirm the ORR remains at the triple-phase boundary.\n","COMMENT":"ORR follows the free surface. OER remains wetted. Flooding and dry-out flags stay\nclear under slow height change.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-FLOAT"},"280":{"MID":"358edf7794fd40da984b8b6e3dadc281","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-SPIRAL","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Spiral bifilar iron and OER","STATEMENT":"Assemble spiral iron and anode current collector with spiral OER at least\npartially bifilar and ORR facing the spiral axis (EP4602674A1 Claim 70).\n","COMMENT":"Iron and OER remain interdigitated along the spiral. ORR air face is toward the\naxis of rotation. Electrolyte contacts iron, ORR first surface, and OER.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-SPIRAL"},"281":{"MID":"f9749b04dec34f25a4a195a2b7bf9655","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-PLEAT","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Pleated iron and OER","STATEMENT":"Operate pleated iron and pleated OER with channels between pleats\n(EP4602674A1 Claim 77; FIG. 17), optionally with OER on both iron faces\n(Claim 80).\n","COMMENT":"Channels exist between pleats. OER occupies channels perpendicular to the ORR\nplane. Dual-face OER is a selectable option.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-PLEAT"},"282":{"MID":"0e427531c625445f9924887fa39498dc","UID":"IA-SCN-PAT-PO2","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Adjustable oxygen partial pressure","STATEMENT":"Raise pO2 on discharge within 0.01-100 atm and lower pO2 on charge within\n0.001-100 atm (US12308414B2 pressurized-gas paragraph).\n","COMMENT":"Discharge driving force increases with pO2. Charge OER is not forced to high pO2.\nSolver remains within property-model domain.\n","_LINK":"IA-SCN-PAT-PO2"},"283":{"MID":"36f047542dfb4d46918f861f46eac25c","UID":"SEC-TD-FOUND","TITLE":"Foundation and chemistry tests","_LINK":"SEC-TD-FOUND"},"284":{"MID":"93265f2345854f4d941d7ea901faa7f5","STATEMENT":"These test descriptions specify how phase-gate and patent-mapped requirements are\nexecuted. Procedures operate on the ironair package. They do not energize physical\ncells from US12308414B2 or EP4602674A1.\n","_LINK":"93265f2345854f4d941d7ea901faa7f5"},"285":{"MID":"d105731288f3435fbcc72e045c5a05e8","UID":"IA-TD-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Package import test","STATEMENT":"Import ironair and the public subpackages listed in IA-SYS-001.\n","COMMENT":"No ImportError. __version__ is a nonempty string.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-001"},"286":{"MID":"4d5fd6add2d249e68515d995eae13acb","UID":"IA-TD-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Constants immutability test","STATEMENT":"Read F_C_MOL, R_J_MOL_K, T_REF_K, P_REF_PA, M_FE_KG_MOL, M_O2_KG_MOL, M_H2O_KG_MOL,\nand M_KOH_KG_MOL. Attempting assignment shall fail.\n\nConfirm T_REF_K corresponds to 25 C and P_REF_PA to 1 atm per US12308414B2\nstandard ambient temperature and pressure.\n","COMMENT":"Values match documented CODATA/IUPAC constants. Frozen assignment raises.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-002"},"287":{"MID":"1f403399d3bf47f080f32e11ebfa6d58","UID":"IA-TD-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Elemental conservation test","STATEMENT":"For each column of S, compute the elemental residual E @ S[:, j] for Fe, O, H, K,\nand C, including Fe/Fe(OH)2, Fe(OH)2/Fe3O4, ORR, OER, and HER columns from\nIA-SYS-020.\n","COMMENT":"Absolute residual < 1e-12 for Fe, O, H, K, and C.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-003"},"288":{"MID":"0c619c7c08724fa1a1e7afd0fb301ab0","UID":"IA-TD-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Butler-Volmer equilibrium test","STATEMENT":"Evaluate electrode current at zero overpotential with finite activities for iron,\nORR, OER, and HER couples.\n","COMMENT":"Net Faradaic current is ~0. Symmetric coefficients produce an odd i(eta) function.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-004"},"289":{"MID":"21f630ea04594c52ba44b429988aeffc","UID":"SEC-TD-ARCH","TITLE":"Component, traceability, and patent-architecture tests","_LINK":"SEC-TD-ARCH"},"290":{"MID":"351340fce23540519fe9eeee50ba5a0c","UID":"IA-TD-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"RHS determinism test","STATEMENT":"Evaluate rhs twice with identical arguments for every DynamicComponent, including\nIronElectrode in DRI-bed and channeled modes, OxygenReduction in stacked submerged\nmode, and OxygenEvolution in interdigitated mode.\n","COMMENT":"Arrays compare equal. Component parameter values are unchanged.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-005"},"291":{"MID":"17d0637beb17405d8cccf1444b8cf97c","UID":"IA-TD-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Traceability test","STATEMENT":"Parse reqs/**/*.sdoc UIDs and src/**/*.py @relation markers.\n\nPatent-mapped UIDs IA-SYS-006 through IA-SYS-026, IA-CHM-011, IA-SDD-006 through\nIA-SDD-010, IA-IRS-006 through IA-IRS-010, IA-TP-004 through IA-TP-006,\nIA-TD-007 through IA-TD-010, and IA-SCN-PAT-* SHALL exist in StrictDoc.\n","COMMENT":"Every @relation UID exists. Every implementation requirement listed in the\ntraceability map has a function marker.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-006"},"292":{"MID":"b93ee7dd1f37422bac26d09fe701174a","UID":"IA-TD-007","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Dual-electrode switch test","STATEMENT":"Place IronAirCell in charge mode and confirm the electrical path is iron-OER-source\nwith ORR isolated from OER potential. Switch to discharge and confirm iron-ORR-load\n(EP4602674A1 [0049]; US12308414B2 FIGS. 5A-5B).\n","COMMENT":"Mode is discrete. ORR is not held at OER potential during charge when isolation\nis enabled.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-007"},"293":{"MID":"c0b6176f4ed74cffa2590def0de01cd5","UID":"IA-TD-008","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Stacked ORR equal pressure-drop test","STATEMENT":"Build a vertical stack of discrete submerged ORR sections with depth-varying\ngeometry. Compute algebraic pressure drop in the depth direction\n(US12308414B2 Claim 1).\n","COMMENT":"Pressure drops compare equal within solver tolerance. Geometry may still vary\nwith depth.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-008"},"294":{"MID":"c30b312a67c44857aa1fb687d6024294","UID":"IA-TD-009","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Interdigitated coulombic-efficiency trend test","STATEMENT":"Run matched planar, channeled, and interdigitated layouts at 6 M KOH, 303 K,\nChanFrac 0.4, w_rib 5 mm, and 4 g Fe/cm2 (EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-27 conditions).\nCompare instantaneous coulombic charge efficiency and discharge voltage plateau\nlength.\n","COMMENT":"Interdigitated efficiency is greater than or equal to planar efficiency at\nend-of-charge. Test records architecture flags and does not claim hardware\nvalidation.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-009"},"295":{"MID":"adb7343e24ae4a8184ddf88d7a08d19f","UID":"IA-TD-010","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Electrolyte and loading domain test","STATEMENT":"Construct ParameterSets for 6 M KOH, 5.5 M KOH + 0.5 M LiOH, 6 M NaOH, 5 M NaOH +\n1 M KOH, and 7 M hydroxide, and for iron loading 1 and 7 g Fe/cm2 with electrolyte\nvolume fraction 0.5 and 0.9 (US12308414B2 electrolyte examples; EP4602674A1\nClaims 16-17). Reject a loading of 0 g Fe/cm2 or a volume fraction of 1.1.\n","COMMENT":"In-range sets construct. Out-of-range sets raise a domain error.\n","_LINK":"IA-TD-010"},"296":{"MID":"4b4bda5688de444581da896513067687","UID":"SEC-TP-GATES","TITLE":"Phase-gate and quality strategy","_LINK":"SEC-TP-GATES"},"297":{"MID":"2261d04bc6724ea4b4b09a1edd846350","STATEMENT":"This test plan verifies the ironair software against the SDRS, including\npatent-mapped plant requirements from US12308414B2 and EP4602674A1. Automated\ntests are verification, not experimental validation of Form Energy hardware or\nof the patents themselves (IA-TST-006).\n","_LINK":"2261d04bc6724ea4b4b09a1edd846350"},"298":{"MID":"069e38c9fa464125b9d13225ced31482","UID":"IA-TP-001","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Phase-gate testing","STATEMENT":"Each development phase SHALL have a passing pytest suite before the phase is complete.\n\nPhase 1: package import, constants, parameters, ODE protocol, StrictDoc UIDs.\nPhase 2: stoichiometric conservation, Butler-Volmer, Nernst, thermodynamics,\nincluding EP4602674A1 Equations 1-2 capacities and US12308414B2 four-electron ORR.\nPhase 3: chemistry notebooks execute.\nPhase 4+: component, coupled, scenario, and conservation tests as implemented,\nincluding dual-electrode switching, stacked-ORR equal-dP algebra, and\ninterdigitated versus planar coulombic-efficiency trends.\n","COMMENT":"make test-phase-1 through make test-phase-2 fail closed when the corresponding tests fail.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-001"},"299":{"MID":"195459a66ebe449a8a0605a10217143e","UID":"IA-TP-002","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Verification versus validation","STATEMENT":"Automated tests verify software against the SDRS. They SHALL NOT be labeled as\nexperimental hardware validation of US12308414B2 or EP4602674A1.\n\nQualitative agreement with EP4602674A1 FIGS. 19-27 trends (interdigitated higher\ncoulombic efficiency and extended discharge plateau versus planar) SHALL be\nreported as model-to-disclosure comparison, not as a claim that unpublished\nexperimental points were reproduced.\n","COMMENT":"README and ironair.validation state this distinction.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-002"},"300":{"MID":"dedcf2c11c664be69ad4fd1fd8c9897c","UID":"IA-TP-003","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"CI quality gates","STATEMENT":"CI SHALL run ruff, mypy, pytest with coverage, StrictDoc validation, and\nrequirements traceability.\n\nStrictDoc export of reqs/ SHALL succeed after patent-section updates. Every\nIA-SYS-006 through IA-SYS-026 and IA-SCN-PAT-* UID SHALL parse.\n","COMMENT":".github/workflows/ci.yml implements these steps.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-003"},"301":{"MID":"167f5af327c84a50bafe1bdf0aeca574","UID":"SEC-TP-PAT","TITLE":"Patent-mapped verification objectives","_LINK":"SEC-TP-PAT"},"302":{"MID":"c87fd626083a456aae540ec1ff6b66b1","UID":"IA-TP-004","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Chemistry verification objectives","STATEMENT":"Tests SHALL confirm Fe/Fe(OH)2 two-electron Faraday mapping, 960 mAh/g first-step\nand 320 mAh/g second-step theoretical capacities as parameter metadata\n(EP4602674A1 [0043]), four-electron ORR/OER, two-electron HER, and elemental\nresiduals below 1e-12.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-004"},"303":{"MID":"90aaad8693df4e40bd643dfc4f12179e","UID":"IA-TP-005","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Architecture verification objectives","STATEMENT":"Tests SHALL instantiate stacked submerged ORR with equal section pressure drop\n(US12308414B2 Claim 1), DRI pellet-bed anode (Claim 18), and channeled\ninterdigitated OER (EP4602674A1 Claim 1) without rhs side effects.\n\nDual-electrode isolation SHALL keep ORR off the OER potential during charge.\nChannel geometry parameters SHALL reject values outside 3-50 mm length and\n1-40 mm width unless a documented override is set.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-005"},"304":{"MID":"27847b1aff004536bf87d44c2245b8b6","UID":"IA-TP-006","STATUS":"Active","TITLE":"Scenario verification objectives","STATEMENT":"The scenario suite SHALL include IA-SCN-PAT-* cases for stacked ORR, DRI bed,\ninterdigitated versus planar comparison, asymmetric 100/300 h duration, floating\nORR, spiral, pleated, and adjustable pO2.\n\nEach scenario SHALL remain a software verification case: conservation, mode\nswitching, and trend direction versus the disclosures, not a hardware ATP.\n","_LINK":"IA-TP-006"}};
